WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.060
- Can I call the board to order?

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.672
Thank you, everybody,

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:10.713
for joining this public
board this afternoon.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.300
Let me start by welcoming you all actually

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.580
to the new NHS England Board,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.060
following our integration
with NHS Improvement

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.140
on the 1st of July.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.720
And I'd just like to say for the minute,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.870
but also personally a sincere thank you

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.200
to Lord Ara Darzi and Lord Patrick Carter

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.270
and Dame Julia Goodfellow

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.880
for their service to the
NHS Improvement Board.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.490
And their contributions
have been invaluable,

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.283
and we are sad to lose them.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:43.950
Following on from that,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.160
you may well have seen that
we have been advertising

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.600
for new non-executive
directors to join the board.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.450
The application process has now closed

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.400
and we had a good number,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.070
a significant number of applications,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.930
and we're in the process
of shortlisting those,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.740
and interviews are currently
planned to take place

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.333
before the end of July.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.360
The Health and Care Act

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.350
has now established the
integrated care systems in law,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.050
and therefore further
enabling local leaders

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.840
to transform the way
that treatment and care

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.030
is delivered across the country.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.090
One reflection I would have
is that we at the centre

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.650
must strike a balance,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.280
ensuring we empower and delegate to,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.010
but we empower the
integrated care systems,

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.770
and without being too
prescriptive in our guidance,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.023
so establishing the right balance.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.340
Let me welcome Chris Hopson,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.140
who has recently joined the executive team

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.123
as our Chief Strategy Officer.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.340
And I'm looking forward to the fact

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.680
that you're going to help me
schedule regular engagement

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.530
actually with the integrated
care system chairs.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.300
And I very much look forward

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.340
to working with them very closely.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.193
And Chris, your help
there would be invaluable.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.430
And I'd just like to reflect
very briefly on an announcement

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.810
that was made probably only
about 45 minutes ago internally.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.880
And that was an announcement about

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.050
how we intend to manage the
integration and merger with,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:16.883
hi, Julian,

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:21.000
with NHS Digital and HEE,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.280
as well as also involving

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.060
and bringing to ourselves
the people from X.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.040
And Amanda is going to talk about that

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.770
when we go to the Chief
Executive's report.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.600
But it's a very important step forward

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.220
for the way the board
actually is going to run,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.430
the new NHS England is going to run.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.410
And we clearly had that conversation

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.180
in a private board this morning,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.480
and I'm very grateful
for the board's support,

00:02:45.348 --> 00:02:47.640
more than support actually,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.640
wholehearted support
for the new structure.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:57.710
We're also going to
launch a further review

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:02.070
around what we call sort of
effectiveness efficiency,

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.870
and where we've asked Jeremy Townsend,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.640
I'm very grateful to Jeremy,

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.620
who's going to lead that review

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.410
to see how we can find more effective ways

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.860
of managing down through into the systems.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.898
And again, Amanda will
will comment upon that.

00:03:17.898 --> 00:03:21.557
Now I should say, as a matter
of logistics that, again,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.530
this is a board which is in public,

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.610
so we are clearly streaming
this board meeting.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:30.900
I'm hoping that by the time

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.050
we do the next one of
these boards actually

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:36.030
that the members of the
public who wish to attend

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.463
will actually be able
to attend physically,

00:03:39.297 --> 00:03:43.697
rather than simply see us
here in action on the screen.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.520
I have got apologies for this meeting

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.273
from Rakesh Kapoor and from Wol Kolade.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.750
And Wol was with us in
this morning's board,

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.350
but unfortunately could
not be here this afternoon.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.660
Before I hand over to
Amanda, very importantly,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.470
can I just ask whether anybody
has any conflict of interest

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.473
that they need to declare
for this board meeting?

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.013
David?

00:04:10.013 --> 00:04:11.730
- I think I probably need to declare

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.820
I'm the Chair of Health Education England

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.849
in light of the announcement
at two o'clock today.

00:04:17.849 --> 00:04:19.075
Thank you.

00:04:19.075 --> 00:04:19.908
- And Laura?

00:04:19.908 --> 00:04:22.293
- I will do the same for NHS Digital.

00:04:22.293 --> 00:04:24.550
- Very good, thank you.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.960
And Navina has also sent her apologies

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.540
for this meeting this afternoon.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.520
So at that point,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.080
let me just reflect.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.213
This morning's board, I thought,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.530
involved a series of very
important discussions,

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.060
the one I've just referenced,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.550
which Amanda will talk to the merger

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:52.550
of the main entities in NHS,

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:57.790
so Digital, X and HEE coming
alongside NHS England.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.520
But we also had a very good conversation,

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.120
which we're going to, I
think dig further into

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.130
around actually some of
the significant pressures

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.020
we're facing at the moment.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.700
And this morning,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.500
the board expressed its concern

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.900
around the amount of
pressure that we can see

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.700
whether it's in the elective backlog,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.750
whether it's bed capacity,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.580
whether it's pressure on workforce,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.180
urgent and emergency care response times,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.400
and also access to primary.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.570
So this afternoon, David,

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.100
I think you're going to be leading for us,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.340
I think, an operational
performance update again.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.010
These are clearly very
significantly challenging times.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.720
And again, thank you to the board

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.520
for this morning's
engagement on that topic.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.460
And again, I think we have
further exploration of that

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.010
to do this afternoon.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.370
It leads me to one
theme, which is capacity,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.383
and I keep using this single word.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.810
And each board meeting now,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.150
it's my intention that
we will have a heading

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.730
for a discussion, which is capacity.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.130
And whether it's going
to be workforce capacity

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:00.120
or whether it's physical
capacity in terms of hospitals,

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.520
or in beds or in scanners,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.810
or in working practises,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.730
or the application of technology,

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.880
I think it's really important
that we engage at every board

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.583
on the question of capacity.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.610
And then lastly, as I
settle into virtually

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:18.330
now my third month I think as Chair

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.560
I remain very keen,

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.630
it's a personal message for me to engage

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.440
with patients directly and
with staff in the frontline

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.950
as firmly and directly as I can.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.410
And since the last board meeting,

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.910
a number of visits and meetings I had,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.010
I was perhaps most
privileged, I think, to visit,

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:38.670
it was a Central London
Community Healthcare Trust,

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:39.540
actually where I met

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.390
actually frankly quite
wonderful community nurses.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.387
And they did actually show me

00:06:44.387 --> 00:06:46.920
and share with me the very real challenges

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.180
that they face actually
in looking after patients.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.800
And I learned a huge amount

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.350
about the way actually
the NHS participates

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.190
and plays such a good role
in supporting community care.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.320
We finished the day actually with a visit

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.680
to a homeless centre,

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.630
the Edward Alsop hostel,

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.240
where actually we are providing care,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.770
they are providing care directly

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:14.943
to some of our most vulnerable
and marginalised groups.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.720
And then I just finished
by saying it was fantastic

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.610
to actually join NHS
Confederation up in Liverpool

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.320
at the expo last month,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.531
where Amanda made I'd say a brilliant

00:07:28.531 --> 00:07:30.510
and actually inspirational speech.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.470
And for those members of the board

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.780
who haven't had the chance to read it,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.430
I would encourage you to read it

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.440
for the clarity of its vision.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.780
And it did use a rather
good sort of prompt,

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.223
which is four Rs,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.680
so the four Rs,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.510
and you'll probably do that again.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.210
But actually it was a
very good central spine

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.990
around which the speech was
was developed and built,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.190
but it was an excellent,

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:54.600
excellent speech.
- Thank you.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.810
- Now, let me pause and see
if there are any comments

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.120
any board members want to make
about the forthcoming agenda

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.920
we're going to look at today.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.110
And in particular, I'm
welcoming Jacqui Rock,

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:05.550
is going to talk to us actually

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.140
about the new central commercial function,

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.890
and Tim with regard to the NHS app.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.650
So, any comments or questions?

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.933
Or shall we head straight
into the meeting?

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.239
Amanda, over to you for
the Chief Exec's update.

00:08:20.239 --> 00:08:22.393
- Thank you very much, indeed.

00:08:22.393 --> 00:08:25.860
So to build on what Richard said,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.180
I think we've got a very, very busy agenda

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:31.680
reflecting the range of things

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.360
that are going on at the moment,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.670
both within NHS England
as an organisation,

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.830
and of course within the wider NHS.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.810
I just wanted to start
by saying, of course,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.790
thank you to everybody who has worked

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:46.020
on the legal merger of NHS
Improvement and NHS England.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.930
That has now happened so
as of the 1st of July,

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.730
so particularly thanks to Steve Power,

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.850
who's led NHSI through that process.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.920
And also just to echo Richard, if I may,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.630
and on behalf, certainly
the executive team,

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:01.650
your thanks to Lords Darzi
and Carter and to Dame Julia

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.480
for their work on the board.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.370
I think we have really, really valued

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:09.420
and appreciated their support
and challenge over the years.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.780
So I did want just to pick up on your cue,

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:13.620
Richard, thank you,

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.920
to say a word about where we are now

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.100
in relation then to the next
stage of the development

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.080
of this organisation,

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.770
which we discussed in
some detail this morning

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:26.910
with the board.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:30.600
And I wrote two members
of this organisation

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.220
a short while ago,

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.360
and actually HEE and NHS
Digital colleagues as well,

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.360
to update them on the work towards this.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.730
And we are intending to write out formerly

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:44.970
to system leaders later today as well.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:48.540
So in my note, I set out the opportunity

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.850
that I think we now have

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.390
to shape and design our
combined organisation

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.060
around the drivers for change

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.250
that we have collectively discussed

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:00.900
over the last few months,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.840
the opportunity that we
have to come together

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.400
as a new NHS England,

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.504
putting data digital technology

00:10:07.504 --> 00:10:09.300
at the heart of how we work,

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.230
and also, of course,

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:12.870
the opportunity to put
workforce at the heart

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.470
of the way that we operate
as a national organisation,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:17.430
which reflects, of course,

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:18.750
the way that workforce sits at the heart

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.730
of everything we do as in NHS.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:23.550
But also the point that
you made about the fact

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.660
that we now have statutory
recognition of ICSs.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.120
And that gives us both,
I think, an opportunity,

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.350
but also it gives us the responsibility

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.860
to think about how we shape
our national organisation

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.310
so that we are best supporting
and enabling systems.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.910
So we this morning redefined,

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.880
I think this is the
sort of third big point,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:52.110
redefined our commitment
to an overarching purpose,

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.590
which now will guide the
next steps of our work

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:59.340
on the detailed design for
the combined organisation.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:00.630
And just to say that out loud,

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.910
that was to lead the NHS in England

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:06.120
to deliver high-quality services for all.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.330
So central to achieving
that ambition, of course,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:12.660
is to provide the best
possible support that we can,

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.540
where we can really add value
as a national organisation

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.640
to make a real difference for patients

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.130
and for the people that
we serve across the NHS.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.500
So what do we need to do?

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.610
We need to reduce the size
of the combined NHS England

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:28.950
and be rigorous about what we do.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:31.920
We need to simplify how
we work with each other

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.390
and the wider system.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:35.760
And we need to ensure
our resources are used

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.090
as effectively and
efficiently as possible.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.400
So in designing the new NHS England,

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.410
and I've written to colleagues about this,

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.950
we will look at what
activity we undertake.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:50.820
We will look at what functions
we can delegate to ICSs,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:51.653
but we will,

00:11:51.653 --> 00:11:53.277
and I think this is a
personal commitment for me,

00:11:53.277 --> 00:11:56.820
but I know echoed by colleagues
across the board table,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.090
we will continue to do
this in a co-produced way.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:04.260
So we will seek to co-produce
with systems externally,

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.850
our colleagues in ICSs,

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.170
our frontline colleagues,

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.510
and we will work
internally with colleagues

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:12.000
over the next few months

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.640
as we do that detailed design work.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.490
So what does that mean for us though?

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:19.530
So based on the initial work,

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.590
we do expect that by the end of 2023, '24,

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.510
our new single organisation will be

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:29.040
at least 30% and up to 40% smaller

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.290
than the current combined
size of NHS England,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:34.473
NHS Digital, and Health Education England.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:40.140
I think just want to be
really upfront and say,

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.410
we do recognise that that is
a difficult message for staff

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.170
within our own three organisations,

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:49.080
and that it does have the
potential to create uncertainty.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.080
But I think are also a
clear sense as a board

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.550
from this morning's conversation

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:56.400
is that it is right to
be upfront with people.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.460
And now as we get into
the detailed design,

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.170
we're going to need that wider engagement.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.050
So being clear,

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:07.650
being straightforward about
the drivers for change,

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.570
about our vision and
ambitions for the future,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.070
about the kind of organisation

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.630
and the ambitions we have,

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:13.740
both for our culture,

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:15.420
as well as for our shape and size,

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:19.230
will now shape our engagement from now on.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:22.290
So we've set out a
clear set of commitments

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.450
to ensure for colleagues internally

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:25.917
they're aware of what support is available

00:13:25.917 --> 00:13:27.297
and how to access it.

00:13:27.297 --> 00:13:29.100
And as I say, our
commitment now, of course,

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.570
is to work with people as we get into

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:32.250
the next stage of design.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.730
Mark, Chair, might want to
pick up on this further.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:37.920
But do you want me to
carry on on my remarks?

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.690
And then we'll come back.
- I think after you finish,

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:42.000
I'll bring Mark in to talk
a little bit more about,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.230
I imagine, that sort of thing.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:44.063
- Yeah, thank you.

00:13:44.063 --> 00:13:46.770
And if I could just, once
again, put on record,

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.180
my thanks to Mark Cubbon,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:50.070
who is the SRO for this piece of work

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:51.150
on behalf of the executive,

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:54.090
but also for the involvement
that we've had from David,

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:55.860
from Laura, from Wol,

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.510
giving real direction and support,

00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.670
particularly from a
non-executive perspective

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:02.070
has been completely invaluable.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.030
So if I just move on

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:05.130
to the other things I
wanted to cover, if I may,

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:09.060
so to move on to a couple
of other notable things.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.790
So we have had, I think,

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.520
two really big causes of celebration

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.190
over the course of the last week.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.320
So the 74th birthday of the NHS,

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:20.820
which was on the 5th.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.040
I was delighted to be
able to spend some time

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:24.330
with the Youth Forum

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:29.130
and also out at Charing
Cross Hospital to mark that.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:31.650
It's also been confirmed that the NHS

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:36.650
will physically receive the George Cross

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.090
next week at Windsor Castle

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:43.140
in recognition of the courage,
the compassion and the skill

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.980
of all of those people who
contributed to the NHS response

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:49.290
over the last two and a half years.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:52.770
So that is timely as it comes
together with the birthday,

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.530
and we're absolutely
delighted, once again,

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.660
to be able to shine a light
on the astonishing work

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.180
that the NHS has done and its partners

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:01.740
over the last couple of years.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.390
Second thing, you've already
mentioned it Richard,

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:06.990
we celebrated, this is not
a real word, but ICS Day,

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:08.610
I think we can call it
now, on Friday the 1st.

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:13.610
And I was out in Crawley
visiting teams there

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.920
just to see, again, actually
the work that they're doing

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.260
on the grant is already
making such a big difference,

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.230
being able to bring together
members of the NHS family,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.090
but also with wider social care partners,

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.473
voluntary sector partners.

00:15:27.158 --> 00:15:30.690
And at Crawley Town Football Club,

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:34.530
I saw that it's not just words, it's real,

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.020
and it was just great to be part of that.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:38.670
You mentioned Richard, thank you,

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.180
the ConfedExpo conference.

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:46.766
And I thought, given that you
gave me such a kind cue in,

00:15:46.766 --> 00:15:50.940
I would just unpack a little bit

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.190
what we discussed at ConfedExpo.

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:54.690
It was a great opportunity

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:56.940
to come face to face with colleagues

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.500
to collectively get around

00:15:58.500 --> 00:15:59.333
I think some of the really big things

00:15:59.333 --> 00:16:00.563
that are going on in the NHS,

00:16:00.563 --> 00:16:03.060
some of the challenges,
some of the opportunities,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:07.120
but also to get, I think,
a real sense of momentum

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.770
behind how we can come together

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:14.250
as a leadership community
with our partners.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.900
And we talked about four Rs,

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.870
I talked about four Rs in my speech,

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:22.320
and they were recovery, reform,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.770
resilience and respect.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.010
Recovery, and this is a
theme that David will pick up

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.670
in the operational
performance paper shortly,

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.370
recovery isn't just about the obvious

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:36.990
elective recovery programme,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.780
although we should note the huge progress

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.570
on reducing patients who
have waited over two years.

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:46.290
So down 80% from their peak in January.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.210
And again, I think a huge amount,

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.910
not just of hard work,
but of real creativity,

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.850
new pathways, new services
that have been put in place

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:54.990
to respond to that challenge.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:58.320
But it's also that recovery
challenge in primary care,

00:16:58.320 --> 00:16:59.610
in urgent emergency care,

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:02.520
in mental health, in community services.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:05.310
And we'll come on to talking
about some of that shortly.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:08.550
Reform, it's the obvious
opportunity we have now

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.050
around system working.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.360
It's a big theme for me in that,

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.793
but also embedding tech and data,

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.730
including, so great that we've
got the opportunity today

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:20.970
to talk about the app as
another really practical example

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:23.280
of where I think we can see

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:26.250
that it's not just about doing
things like we did it before,

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.140
it's very much about
doing things differently

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.630
as we have done throughout the
whole of the last two years,

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.390
even more so than the 74 years of the NHS,

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.590
which again has been
characterised by change

00:17:38.175 --> 00:17:40.140
across its life span.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:43.080
Resilience, you've
mentioned already, Richard,

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.820
and I strongly agree

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:48.840
that we do need to have a really
clear view on our capacity,

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.840
and what we really need to be as efficient

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.210
and as productive as we need to be.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:56.060
And there is a point where,

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.370
I think, I said it in my speech last week,

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:02.400
the efficiency we have shown over recent,

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:03.780
well, over a long period of time,

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:05.970
to get down to the bed
base we currently carry,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:07.680
risks becoming inefficient,

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.770
if we don't have the head space
we need to meet the demand.

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:12.990
That certainly capacity part of it,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.090
but workforce the other critical part

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:17.670
of that resilience piece.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:20.760
And we've got a great opportunity
with the workforce plan

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.420
to do some really good thinking,
not just about numbers,

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:27.120
but about types of roles, shapes of roles,

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:29.160
the workforce of the future,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:34.110
but it's a hugely important
component of resilience.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.400
And then finally, respect.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.740
And I've talked about
that in three dimensions,

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.110
respect for our current staff.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:41.760
I've just talked about
how that will characterise

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:44.550
our approach to our own
internal change process.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.770
But of course, that's true across the NHS

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:51.270
and thinking about how
we firmly put leadership

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:52.620
in the front and centre,

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.560
our response to the messenger
review will be part of that.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:59.130
But also how we support
equality, diversity, inclusion,

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.410
very much at the core of
how we work with colleagues.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.200
Secondly, respect for our patients.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.110
So that is really leaning
into the quality agenda

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.037
and our ability to just keep saying,

00:19:09.037 --> 00:19:10.680
"What matters to you?"

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:12.810
rather than, "What is
the matter with you?"

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:14.370
So that personalization agenda

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:16.410
at the heart of the respect agenda.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:17.900
And then finally, of course,

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:19.980
it is respect for taxpayers

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:23.580
because ensuring we are
delivering value for money

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.190
and efficiency is a critical part,

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:29.160
I think, of our responsibility
as a public service

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:30.720
and our responsibility to taxpayers.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.610
So we talked about those four Rs,

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:33.810
they seem to have resonated

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.610
as I've talked to
colleagues across the NHS,

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.860
and we'll keep coming back to a structure,

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:41.430
I think, over time
around those four areas,

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:42.990
some of which is very much here and now,

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:44.430
some of which is for the longer term,

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:46.860
but I think hopefully a helpful framework.

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.710
If I may, I can just
jump off on that point

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.020
about efficiency and
respect for taxpayers,

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:53.850
just to say a word more as promised

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:55.440
about the work that Jeremy,

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:59.790
very grateful that you
have agreed to lead for us.

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:01.740
As we know, the NHS is already one

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.350
of the most efficient health
services in the world.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:05.520
And before the pandemic,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:08.460
we consistently outperformed
the wider public sector

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.520
and UK economy on productivity.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:12.840
And we really appreciate,

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:15.150
Julian, I'm sure will say
more about this later,

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:16.530
the enormous amount of work

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:20.040
that's gone into local system plans,

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:23.400
finding over five billion
pounds worth of savings

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.810
to be delivered this year

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:28.320
in the context of an extremely
challenging environment.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.840
And we'll talk about
COVID again in a moment,

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:32.220
but I think it is really important

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:37.220
that what we now do is ask
ourselves continuously,

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:42.000
but certainly we use the
opportunity of Jeremy's work

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.520
to say, "What more can we do nationally

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.620
to help systems deliver
further efficiencies

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:47.880
over the coming years?"

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.860
Including how we recover the progress

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:53.100
that was made in some
areas such as agency spend,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.190
which were inevitably set
back during the pandemic.

00:20:56.190 --> 00:21:00.360
So Jeremy, you will bring
considerable expertise to this.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.940
I think we're calling it
an efficiency stock-take,

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:04.587
and work closely with Julian

00:21:04.587 --> 00:21:06.660
and other members of the executive,

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:08.730
including Jacqui, in her capacity,

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:10.440
leading our commercial work of course,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.060
to collate what's already going on

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.160
and where the opportunities are.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:16.380
So continuing to build
on the gains we've made

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:17.940
in recent years on procurement

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.830
will be an important part of this.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.430
Jacqui's going to lead a discussion

00:21:23.828 --> 00:21:26.130
on the commercial work
shortly, so very timely.

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.290
And of course, how we
use data and technology

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:30.780
to automate and speed up processes.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:34.710
We know we can get into really
hard yards on efficiency,

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.990
but we should always remember

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:38.610
that finding that headroom

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:41.523
means being able to
reinvest in those things

00:21:41.523 --> 00:21:43.410
that we want to achieve for patients,

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:45.060
whether that's new treatments,

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:48.210
prevention programmes,
or improved services.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:49.770
So that was what I
wanted to say about that.

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:51.840
Thank you, Jeremy.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:54.810
So final couple of things,

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:56.430
we are really aware,

00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:57.930
and again, David will say more about this,

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:00.090
that there has been a
very significant increase

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.590
in COVID infections.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.240
In fact, Steve was just updating us

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:05.820
on the most recent data this morning

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:08.880
that has clearly had an
impact on staff sickness

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:13.680
and also an impact on our
capacity in hospitals,

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.690
as well as primary care
community services,

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:17.760
mental health, of course.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.300
We've also been talking
with colleagues at UKHSA

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.820
about what they're
seeing over in Australia

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:25.620
with the flu season there.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:30.620
And we are conscious that
there is a likely impact

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:32.700
from some of the cost of living pressures

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:35.610
on people's need for care,

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.350
particularly over winter.

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:41.370
So a really important piece
of work on planning ahead,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:42.690
planning for winter,

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:44.880
seeking to do as much as we possibly can.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:47.070
And again, I just want to thank
colleagues across the system

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:49.350
'cos I know they've been
heavily involved in this work

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:52.750
over recent weeks to
make sure that we are in

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:57.000
as proactive a position
as we possibly can be

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.670
to respond well to these pressures.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:01.920
And we're also just waiting to hear news,

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:05.580
hopefully reasonably
shortly around the specifics

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.410
of the autumn COVID booster.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.590
So JCVI will advise
all that in due course.

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:12.150
So that was just a kind
of by way of introduction

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.620
to the operational conversation
we're going to have

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:15.090
in a moment.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:16.080
Pay,

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:20.550
we are expecting the NHS pay
settlement at some point soon.

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:21.900
As colleagues will know,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.860
the pay review bodies make a
recommendation to ministers,

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:29.190
and it is then for
ministers to consider that.

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.590
We have planned for a
certain level of uplift.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:35.460
That was 3%, it was baked
into the spending review.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.610
But we are discussing now how any uplift

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:41.760
over above that would be funded.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:43.140
So final point for me,

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:45.120
that might be something
we want to come back to

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:46.500
with Julian later,

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:50.040
but final point for me is
just worth saying very briefly

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:51.480
that the final terms of reference

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:55.020
for the COVID-19 UK-wide public inquiry

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:56.040
have been published.

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:57.960
So it is formally established now

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:01.230
under the Inquiries Act of 2005.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.020
Clearly, we welcome the
opportunity to tell our story

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:06.540
and talk about the remarkable
system-wide efforts

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:08.880
that went into the COVID-19 response.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:11.910
And we will be cooperating
fully with the Inquiry,

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:13.530
both as NHS England,

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.500
but also in supporting
colleagues across the system,

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:18.900
providing helpful information and guidance

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:22.200
on our future NHS site.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.060
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:28.650
- Before I ask Mark to comment
on the merger announcement

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:30.480
and the integration announcement,

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:32.880
are there any comments
or questions for Amanda

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:34.380
on anything that she has said?

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:38.943
Thank you, so Mark,

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:42.150
further reflections in terms
of the work that you did,

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.370
and thank you very much for
all the work that you did

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.033
leading for the execs,
working very closely with Wol,

00:24:46.033 --> 00:24:48.330
and with a number of
the other board members.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.730
In particular, actually, as Amanda said,

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:51.720
David and Laura,

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:56.720
as the respective Chairs
of HEE and Digital.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:59.970
Mark, further reflections on the work?

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:00.803
- Thank you, Chair.

00:25:00.803 --> 00:25:02.160
So just a few additional comments

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:05.100
to build on what you and
Amanda have already said.

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.091
I think it's important just to note

00:25:06.091 --> 00:25:08.610
that when we started
off with this programme,

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:12.600
we did not set a target
size at that point in time.

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:14.910
We wanted to make sure that we spent time

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.130
hearing about things that work really well

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.020
in our three organisations,

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:20.670
and work out some of the things

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.110
that we actually need to change

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:23.760
in terms of how we coordinate

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:26.370
and become more effective
as an organisation,

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:28.260
but how we streamline our interface

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.000
and our connection with the service,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.170
which is what we've done.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:34.110
We've also done a very
extensive baselining exercise

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:36.960
to understand more about the
functions that we provide

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:38.970
in each part of our organisations,

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.910
and more about the roles our people have.

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.190
So that's given us a
really clear impression

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:45.900
in terms of where the
greatest opportunities are

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:48.000
to be more effective

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.160
in terms of how we work
and support transformation

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:51.573
across the service.

00:25:54.681 --> 00:25:55.560
I think it's really important,

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.660
we've done quite an extensive
engagement exercise,

00:25:57.660 --> 00:25:59.340
Dominic Dodd and I have
been doing a piece of work

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:00.540
for a little while,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.670
looking at our future operating framework

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:04.500
for the new organisation.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:07.050
And we've met with about 3 or 400 people

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:07.883
through that process,

00:26:07.883 --> 00:26:10.230
and I think we've heard loud and clear

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:13.770
that we want to make sure
that we are responding to,

00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:16.590
you know, sometimes one
part of our organisation,

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.660
not really knowing what
the other side was doing,

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:23.310
and having multiple interfaces
as we attempt to support

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:26.040
and as we aim to support improvement.

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:27.350
And we've said, actually,

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:28.440
we need to do a better job at that

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.990
in terms of coordinating the activities,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:33.570
and indeed having better line of sight

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:35.430
around the key priorities that we have

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:37.020
and that we place upon,

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:39.270
and ask the service to respond to.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:40.440
So our ask of the service

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:42.360
of the need to speak with one voice

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:43.200
has been loud and clear

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.390
through that engagement exercise.

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:47.070
So what we're going to be
doing over the next few months

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:49.980
is heading into a more
detailed design phase,

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:52.890
which is obviously
involving many more people,

00:26:52.890 --> 00:26:55.290
working with directorate level colleagues,

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:58.050
and also looking at the
horizontal ways of working

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:01.140
the really important elements
about changing the way,

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:02.910
while the whole range of
programmes that we have,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:05.640
looking at how we can
align where possible,

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.230
and also look at how they're delivered

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.330
in perhaps a more effective way,

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:11.670
working even more closely
with system colleagues

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.260
and our providers.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.940
We're building in EDI principles

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.040
right from the outset for the design

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.110
and right through the change process.

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:20.610
And we'll be working hand in hand

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:23.100
as we have done to date with
our trade union colleagues

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:24.510
to make sure that they have enough time

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:26.580
to support the whole
process, the programme,

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:28.680
and importantly, their members as well.

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:30.720
Clearly at this time with the news

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:32.460
that's only been issued today,

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.470
we want to make sure that we're
supporting all of our staff

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:36.570
who will be reading
this for the first time.

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:38.550
And we want to make
sure that line managers

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:39.930
have the support they need

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.367
in order to provide as many answers

00:27:41.367 --> 00:27:44.520
at this point in time for their teams.

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:46.380
We have an all staff message later today

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:49.140
where we have Amanda, Simon, Navina and I

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:52.110
talking to the whole of our organisations,

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.000
all of the staff who'll be joining,

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:55.710
to set out the next steps in the process.

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:59.310
So engagement with the whole organisation,

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:01.080
with all of our people
is going to be critical.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:05.040
We recognise that we need
to build a future culture

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:07.920
for the new organisation,
learning from the best,

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.650
picking up the good things
that work really well,

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.840
the ways of working that have shown

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.330
a really good practise in
each of our organisations.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:18.840
Build on that, but also address
some of those challenges

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.390
that we've had with some
cultural issues in the past.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:23.019
We're working really
closely with networks,

00:28:23.019 --> 00:28:26.070
working really closely with
our Freedom to Speak Up teams,

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:28.380
and they'll be front and
centre to the work that we do

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:33.000
as we address some of the
cultural programme in the future.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:34.200
That's all I will say at the moment.

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:36.600
I'm happy to take any questions, Richard.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:38.490
- We gave it a really good run through,

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:42.033
I think, in this morning's discussion.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:47.130
So I would simply reflect
actually that I think

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:49.383
it's a really important
step forward in the way

00:28:49.383 --> 00:28:51.653
that NHS England, the new NHS England

00:28:51.653 --> 00:28:55.770
is going to run smaller but expert,

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:56.970
I think is really important.

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:00.165
It's following principles
of simplification.

00:29:00.165 --> 00:29:03.120
It's following principles of empowerment

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.480
and empowerment of and
delegation to the systems,

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:08.280
integrated care systems.

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.590
It has a debureaucratisation field

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.360
just in terms of the number of programmes

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:17.010
that we run across these central entities.

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:18.690
And so that air traffic control unit,

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.873
that coordination unit
reporting director, Amanda,

00:29:20.873 --> 00:29:24.810
I think is a very important feature of it.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.550
But just again, I want to thank you,

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:29.100
but to actually to encourage
all of the execs here,

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:33.960
we have to go after this
process as effectively

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:35.970
and as efficiently and as sympathetically

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:37.590
as we possibly can.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:40.693
And speed and pace is
really important for people

00:29:40.693 --> 00:29:43.500
and staying in good
communication with our staff

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:45.330
is also really, really important

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:48.750
as we move through to
the new organisation.

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:50.640
From a board perspective,
again, as you know,

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:53.100
the subcommittee, the board that with whom

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:56.610
you've been working on this
design over the past weeks,

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.360
months actually will stay in position

00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:02.130
and doing a sort that sort
of oversight challenge role,

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:03.780
working with the executives

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:08.073
just as you go through the execution mode.

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:10.410
Any comments, Jeremy?

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:11.550
- I'm just going to make a comment.

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:13.020
I think it's a really good idea.

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:14.965
And I think it's just a good example,

00:30:14.965 --> 00:30:18.218
linking two topics together
of the type of efficiency that

00:30:18.218 --> 00:30:19.493
we're going to try and deliver

00:30:19.493 --> 00:30:21.030
out to the efficiency programme,

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:24.390
removing cost if you like out
the system without impacting,

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:27.960
but hopefully improving patient
care and improving the way

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:31.290
the centre works into the
hospitals and its primary care.

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:33.480
So just linking those two topics together,

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:35.520
it's exactly the kind of
principle that we're looking

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.650
to work through as we look
at other opportunities

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:40.230
for efficiencies within the NHS.

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:41.430
- David.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:44.640
- Richard, just because
this is a public meeting,

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.170
I think it's probably appropriate

00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:48.270
that from a health
education point of view,

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:51.120
that I'd want to welcome this development

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:55.110
to create the new NHS England
to integrate service planning,

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:59.160
financial planning and workforce
planning all in one place.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:02.460
I'm not sure we've been served
as well as we could have been

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:04.680
over the past decade
with this being separate.

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:06.420
So this is a real opportunity, I think,

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.360
to begin to hard wire workforce planning

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:10.470
into the way that we move.

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:14.724
And for that, I think this
is a tremendous opportunity.

00:31:14.724 --> 00:31:16.530
The rubber will hit the road now.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:18.930
We're into this detail and
this news has been released.

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:22.350
So I think the points, Richard
that you, Amanda and Mark

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:24.420
have just emphasised
about supporting staff

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:27.450
is key as we go into the delivery mode,

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:30.480
but from a Health Education
England perspective,

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:34.110
we remain focused on
delivering our commitments

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:36.750
for this year and preparing
the whole organisation

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:38.739
to move into the new NHSE

00:31:38.739 --> 00:31:41.190
from the first of April
next year, thank you.

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.257
- Thank you, and also
thank you for your support

00:31:43.257 --> 00:31:45.450
and work actually on
that board subcommittee,

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:47.610
working with Mark and
other exec colleagues.

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:48.443
Thank you.

00:31:48.443 --> 00:31:49.284
Laura, do you want to comment,

00:31:49.284 --> 00:31:50.700
I mean, from a digital perspective?

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:51.690
- Yes, I was going to say,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:54.990
I think I wear three hats
in this conversation really,

00:31:54.990 --> 00:31:59.990
which is NHS England,
non-exec, Chair of NHS Digital,

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:03.270
but also kind of author of review,

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:06.990
which talked about how did we
set ourselves up in the centre

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:10.290
to actually capitalise
on the digital revolution

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:12.720
and change how we operate.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:17.720
And so I think I welcome
the move to integrate us

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:21.330
and to build a different,

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:25.350
so I think the kind of shaping
of it as a new NHS England

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:28.260
is a really important aspect of this

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:31.680
because I think we need to
be a different organisation

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:33.840
really for kind of two big reasons.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:38.840
One is that we are now
supporting a very different NHS.

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:43.410
Amanda, you were talking about ICS day.

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:45.582
Well, we are now supporting

00:32:45.582 --> 00:32:48.270
a very different world out there,

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:53.023
but equally we need to be
the kind of organisation

00:32:53.023 --> 00:32:57.330
that has the skills and
capability and instinct

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:59.940
to know how to use data and digital

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.560
to its full effectiveness.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.410
And so that does take
us working differently.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:09.030
And so I think that this
is a really important step.

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:14.030
I think I echo David's and
everybody's points about,

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:20.310
we must support our people through this

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:23.220
and we must make sure that as we do this,

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:26.780
we seek to enhance our
digital and data capabilities

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:29.130
rather than do them damage.

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:30.717
And I know we've talked about that

00:33:30.717 --> 00:33:32.859
and I think that everyone is kind of clear

00:33:32.859 --> 00:33:34.920
and I think that's a
really important message.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:38.040
So welcome and want to
make sure that we do

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:40.484
absolutely support our teams and our staff

00:33:40.484 --> 00:33:42.663
to learn to work differently.

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:46.830
- Laura, thank you,
all very good comments.

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:49.260
And Andrew, do you want to comment?

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:50.400
- Yes, if I may.

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:55.380
So I think it's really important
that as we kind of reset

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:59.370
what NHSE does, that we
do that in close concert

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:02.310
with the ICSs and we turn it the other way

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:06.471
and be clear about what is
it from an ICS perspective

00:34:06.471 --> 00:34:09.360
that they're going to look
to the centre for guidance

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:12.900
and what is it that we
are going to allow ICSs

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:15.180
to do on their own.

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:17.190
And I think we need to
get that balance, right?

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.260
'Cos I think over time,

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:23.610
a lot of gravity has moved to
the centre, too much of it,

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:28.590
and people locally have lost
that authority and empowerment.

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:32.070
So I think this reset is really important.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:33.630
We need to get it right,

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:37.307
but I would urge that we
involve the ICS leadership

00:34:37.307 --> 00:34:39.870
right across the service as much as we can

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:43.230
so that we've got the right
balance going forward.

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:45.060
- Richard, I completely agree with Andrew.

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:47.255
So whilst the highlight is
the initial conversations

00:34:47.255 --> 00:34:49.770
we've had about our operating framework,

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:53.100
we need to have a considered engagement

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:54.840
over a period of time
over the next months,

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:55.980
because you know,

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:59.122
as we are already going
down a path of delegation

00:34:59.122 --> 00:35:01.860
with our direct commissioning
functions and then next year,

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:03.931
looking at some of the way that we support

00:35:03.931 --> 00:35:05.220
some of our public health commissioning

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:06.900
and operations functions,

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:09.570
perhaps some specialist
commission services,

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.010
there were other things
in terms of the way

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:12.930
that we deliver our programmes

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:14.610
where we will need to have conversations

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:18.600
with our ICB leadership
community, and then say actually,

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:20.400
are we making the right
assumptions about the best way

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:22.080
to deliver the transformation
that we need to see

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.628
across the service, rather
than let's just hand over

00:35:24.628 --> 00:35:26.280
responsibility for certain things.

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:28.050
So I absolutely agree with Andrew

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:30.657
and will make really strong to do that.

00:35:30.657 --> 00:35:33.450
- And Chris, that's very
resonant to actually the letter

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:34.840
you sent actually about a week or so ago

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:37.590
where you talk about co-creation.

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:38.870
- Yes, I think there's a real
opportunity, isn't there,

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.560
in a number of pieces of work

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:42.870
that we've been doing to really...

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:44.701
As Amanda made the very public commitment

00:35:44.701 --> 00:35:46.500
when we did the leadership conference

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:48.750
with both trust leaders
but also ICS leaders,

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:51.458
about the need as part of this move,

00:35:51.458 --> 00:35:54.950
this big 1st of July
marking an inflexion point

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:58.350
of how we run the NHS,
that we need to co-create.

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:00.210
And I think this is a real opportunity

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:02.373
in terms of both for this work.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:06.990
- Can I at that point and move us on?

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:09.180
I do need, however, to go
back and do a bit logistics,

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:11.550
which is going to just get
the board support to sign off

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:12.890
the minutes, which I--

00:36:14.349 --> 00:36:15.960
A great piece of chairing.

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:17.190
Can I have the board's support

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:20.133
to approve the minutes
of the May public board?

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:22.187
Thank you very much, okay.

00:36:22.187 --> 00:36:25.020
It was obviously a deliberate
mistake on my part.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:26.520
So can we go, David,

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:29.220
I think to operational performance.

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:30.870
- Thank you Richard, and again,

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:32.370
channelling Amanda's four Rs,

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:33.870
I'll certainly be talking a lot about

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:36.015
the twin challenges of
recovery and resilience

00:36:36.015 --> 00:36:37.890
in what is a very challenging time

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:40.530
for colleagues out there in
the service at the moment.

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:42.780
So I think I will begin by
bringing people up to date

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:44.460
with where we are with the COVID pressures

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:45.293
that we're facing.

00:36:45.293 --> 00:36:47.160
You'll notice in the board report,

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:48.840
it says that on the 1st of June,

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:52.200
we had 3,800 patients
in our beds with COVID

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:55.680
and on the 22nd of June, it was 5,720.

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:59.040
Well yesterday it was 11,465.

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:01.719
So we are continuing to
see material pressures

00:37:01.719 --> 00:37:02.850
on the system.

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:05.970
It is likely that that
will increase to 15,000

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:07.830
within the next seven
days, which will be similar

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:10.740
to the peak that we encountered
earlier on in the room.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:13.980
So in the room, earlier on in
the year, sorry colleagues,

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:15.897
that would be dramatic.

00:37:16.950 --> 00:37:19.020
As ever, the main pressure,

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:20.929
a material pressure
that we faced similarly

00:37:20.929 --> 00:37:23.580
earlier on in the year is
the consequential impact

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:25.530
on staff absence.

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:29.415
So staff sickness and absence
is now running at 5.9%,

00:37:29.415 --> 00:37:33.090
1.7% of which is directly
correlated to COVID

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:35.940
and that is having an impact
on operational performance

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:38.011
and recovery, which I will touch on later

00:37:38.011 --> 00:37:40.290
as I work through my report.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:42.360
I thought it would be worth
at this point, Richard,

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.400
bringing in Steve Powis to get his take

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:45.233
on where we are both now

00:37:45.233 --> 00:37:48.090
and as we move into the
weeks and months ahead.

00:37:48.090 --> 00:37:48.923
- Yeah, thanks David.

00:37:48.923 --> 00:37:50.220
So as you said,

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:53.010
we are in another COVID
wave at the moment.

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:56.370
That wave is predominantly
being driven by BA.4 and BA.5,

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:59.790
which are the latest
sub variance of omicron.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:04.770
We had a BA.2 wave in this
spring, peaking in April.

00:38:04.770 --> 00:38:06.720
Other countries have
seen this, in particular,

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:08.220
South Africa and Portugal,

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:12.168
although how any particular country fares

00:38:12.168 --> 00:38:15.480
in a particular wave is very
individual to that country,

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:17.093
and the prior waves they've had

00:38:17.093 --> 00:38:19.593
and the level of immunity
in the population.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:23.220
It's clear that many
other countries though,

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:24.330
are in this wave at the moment.

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:28.080
So we are not the only
country experiencing the wave.

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:30.000
In terms of what will happen, well,

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.980
I think most people in
the epidemiology community

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:37.230
sense that we will
continue to see some rises.

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:39.630
The ONS data I think last
published last Friday

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:41.731
shows that prevalence is still increasing.

00:38:41.731 --> 00:38:46.050
We will continue to see rises
for another maybe week or two,

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:49.080
but I would hope that by the end of July

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:51.030
and maybe a week or two before that,

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:53.092
we will start to see a turning over

00:38:53.092 --> 00:38:55.290
in the prevalence levels in the community.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:59.678
Of course, that will then be
reflected in reduced admissions

00:38:59.678 --> 00:39:02.520
and reduced occupancy.

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:04.740
But there's no doubt
that for the next week,

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:07.980
as David has highlighted and
perhaps sometime after that,

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:10.410
we will continue to
see pressure increasing

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:11.310
before it decreases.

00:39:11.310 --> 00:39:12.750
And of course that's
putting pressure on beds,

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:15.930
but also because of staff
absence because of COVID as well,

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:18.042
which is an very important factor

00:39:18.042 --> 00:39:20.703
in the way we can respond operationally.

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:26.790
Two other points, ITU
numbers as a proportion

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:29.070
of overall patients in hospital with COVID

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:30.780
is still around 3%.

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:34.770
So that is very similar to
what we saw in the spring

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:38.310
and in January as omicron
became established,

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:39.720
but very different from what we've seen

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:42.312
in previous waves last year,

00:39:42.312 --> 00:39:46.440
when the figure was more
typically around 15 to 20%.

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:47.730
So that is good news.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:49.380
People are less severely ill

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:51.630
because of vaccination predominantly,

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:53.670
but also because in part,

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:55.890
because of the variant
we have at the moment.

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:58.524
And the number of people
in hospital with COVID

00:39:58.524 --> 00:40:01.160
as a primary diagnosis
is still around 40%.

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:03.352
It has gone up a bit from around 33%,

00:40:03.352 --> 00:40:06.233
but it's not back at the 70 to 80%

00:40:06.233 --> 00:40:09.570
that it would typically
have been in a year ago.

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:12.321
So, fewer patients being
treated because of COVID,

00:40:12.321 --> 00:40:14.970
but because we still
have patients with COVID,

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:17.250
that is still putting
additional operational pressure

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:19.503
on our health systems.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:23.460
The looking further ahead,

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:26.400
I don't see in my colleagues in UKHSA,

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:28.500
don't see any particular new variant

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:30.180
that's coming through next,

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:32.220
although there are many variants

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:34.290
that they are keeping an eye on.

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:36.251
So once we get through this wave,

00:40:36.251 --> 00:40:40.710
we will have to see how we
fair in August and September,

00:40:40.710 --> 00:40:43.260
but we are expecting as
we go into the winter.

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:44.790
And of course there could be new variants

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:45.623
that come along before,

00:40:45.623 --> 00:40:47.700
but we are expecting as we
get into the winter months,

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:50.490
that we will see increasing
pressure through COVID again.

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:52.650
And as Amanda said earlier,

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:54.870
we're also expecting to see flu this year.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:58.200
We haven't seen significant
incidence of flu

00:40:58.200 --> 00:40:59.970
in the last two winters.

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:01.770
We're keeping a very
close eye on Australia

00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:04.710
where there's been a perhaps
more severe than usual,

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:08.935
perhaps more earlier than usual flu season

00:41:08.935 --> 00:41:10.890
in the Southern hemisphere.

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:14.100
And so that can be a signal
for the Northern hemisphere,

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:15.420
but I think irrespective of that,

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:17.310
we would be very concerned about flu

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:18.540
coming back this winter.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:21.465
And so we are taking that
into account in our planning

00:41:21.465 --> 00:41:23.733
for winter as we will hear in a minute.

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:29.880
Amanda's also mentioned
the vaccine programme,

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:31.590
in particular the COVID vaccine programme.

00:41:31.590 --> 00:41:33.870
But of course, we also have
the flu vaccine programme,

00:41:33.870 --> 00:41:36.360
which begins in September each year.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:38.940
And they're going to be
really important this year,

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:41.356
as important as ever,
even perhaps even more so

00:41:41.356 --> 00:41:45.720
in ensuring that we protect
members of the public,

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:48.360
that we keep demand down
on hospital pressures.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:51.360
So if there's one message for the public,

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:54.150
it's don't take the foot off the pedal

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:56.940
in terms of enthusiasm for vaccination,

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:59.520
both for flu and for COVID.

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:03.300
And if you are in the eligible
groups as determined by JCVI

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:05.791
for either the COVID
vaccine or the flu vaccine

00:42:05.791 --> 00:42:09.900
this autumn, then make sure
that you go and get it.

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:10.890
It will protect you.

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:12.594
It will protect your families,

00:42:12.594 --> 00:42:14.524
particularly from severe illness,

00:42:14.524 --> 00:42:18.113
but it will also help the
NHS in taking pressure

00:42:18.113 --> 00:42:20.820
off the hospitals and
therefore allowing us

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:23.130
to do the work that we do for all patients

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:24.213
with all conditions.

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:27.390
- Excellent, thank you, Steve.

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:30.603
So in terms of the context,
therefore in terms of recovery,

00:42:30.603 --> 00:42:34.620
we remain very focused on
ensuring that on elective care

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:36.630
that we're eliminating all 104 week waits

00:42:36.630 --> 00:42:37.803
by the end of July.

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:40.470
The week of the 26th of June,

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:42.683
there were 4,000, just over 4,500 patients

00:42:42.683 --> 00:42:44.820
who had been waiting more than 104 weeks,

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:48.780
but that was a reduction of
1,130 from the previous week.

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:51.450
And what we are now doing is
ensuring that our recovery

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:54.990
around the 62 day cancer
performance is aligned

00:42:54.990 --> 00:42:57.180
and performance management and supported

00:42:57.180 --> 00:42:58.512
through the same intervention.

00:42:58.512 --> 00:43:00.180
It's very important that you do so

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:01.560
because on the other side of that coin,

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:03.510
you will note in my report
that cancer referrals

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:04.770
continue to go up,

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.600
which is exactly what we would be wanting.

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:08.550
We would be wanting
people to come forward.

00:43:08.550 --> 00:43:10.410
But that means it's behold on us to ensure

00:43:10.410 --> 00:43:12.060
that we can support and manage people

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:14.310
through the diagnostic
and treatment pathway.

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:18.510
I'm quite happy to take questions
on that during discussion.

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:20.040
Two other things I would highlight Richard

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:21.960
before I do throw it open for discussion

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:23.640
is the obvious and apparent pressure

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:25.650
in the urgent care pathway.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.480
And our thoughts at the moment are very,

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:29.310
very much focused as they always are

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:30.840
at this time of the year in ensuring

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:33.990
that we've got a comprehensive
winter plan in place

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:36.577
to see us through the weeks ahead.

00:43:36.577 --> 00:43:39.840
As ever, we'll be focusing
on both admission avoidance,

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.190
both ambulance handovers,

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:44.220
ensuring appropriate flow
through all of our systems.

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:46.200
And we'll be equally
focused as making sure

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:48.180
that the winter plans are in place

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:51.780
for both mental health as
well as physical health.

00:43:51.780 --> 00:43:53.640
Two things I would highlight is firstly,

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:56.730
is our current absolute
focus on discharge.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.610
We've written to all
systems and we are currently

00:43:59.610 --> 00:44:02.970
in the first few days of 100
day sprint to making sure

00:44:02.970 --> 00:44:05.610
that we've got in place the
10 high impact interventions

00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:07.860
that we know make a difference

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:10.140
in terms of moving people
through the system,

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:12.120
ensuring people who don't
need to be in a hospital

00:44:12.120 --> 00:44:13.560
are no longer there.

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:16.680
And secondly, we put out
a call for early pioneers

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:19.020
who are interested in
looking within their system

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.080
about how they, if you throw
the artefacts in the air,

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.360
is there a better, more
radical way of looking

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:25.530
at how you actually move people

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:29.339
through health and social
care systems in the round?

00:44:29.339 --> 00:44:32.670
The final thing I would say
before I open up is again,

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:35.910
to serve Amanda, I think
the second hour of reform,

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:37.968
I need to learn these in the right order.

00:44:37.968 --> 00:44:40.410
Of course, what we also saw of the Confed

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:42.510
was a lot of discussion
about the Fuller Report

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:45.030
and the opportunities that
are now presented to us

00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:48.210
as we move forward in really
looking at how we organise

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:52.560
primary care differently to
ensure both access is addressed,

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:54.870
but also we really get
upstream of people's health

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:57.420
and wellbeing, 'cos it's
much better to stop people

00:44:57.420 --> 00:44:59.151
getting to a hospital in
the first place than working

00:44:59.151 --> 00:45:01.860
at how you get them back out
of the back end of the hospital

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:04.050
if they've fallen or fallen ill.

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:05.580
So I'll pause there, Richard,

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:08.610
to ensure that we've got
sufficient time for discussions.

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:11.313
- So comments or questions,
Andrew, do you want go?

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:14.670
- Thanks, David, that's really helpful.

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:17.970
I think, you know, most
emergency departments

00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:20.550
are under enormous pressure at the moment.

00:45:20.550 --> 00:45:21.930
It'd be good to get your thoughts

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:24.132
on how we might improve capacity.

00:45:24.132 --> 00:45:27.699
I'm talking about bed
capacity in various guises

00:45:27.699 --> 00:45:31.380
going forward and how
we might also, you know,

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:34.080
take a view around ambulance transfers.

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:37.930
'Cos I think if we can do a
bit more on increasing capacity

00:45:37.930 --> 00:45:40.913
in those systems that are
really, really challenged

00:45:40.913 --> 00:45:44.723
and are really, really
struggling to hit 60%

00:45:44.723 --> 00:45:47.250
in terms of the four hour standard,

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:48.480
I know that's a bit of a proxy,

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:52.563
but those that are really,
really in dire straits,

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:56.817
a few things I think that
we could do potentially

00:45:56.817 --> 00:45:59.520
would push them in the right direction.

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:02.550
So it'd be good to get your
thoughts on those two aspects.

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:04.393
- Okay, do you want to
take some questions?

00:46:04.393 --> 00:46:06.420
- If you note those, because I see Munir

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:09.150
is going to ask a
question or make a point,

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:10.740
and Ruth, I think you wanted to.

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:12.010
So Munir.

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:13.197
- Yeah, one question for David

00:46:13.197 --> 00:46:14.640
and one question for Steve if that's okay.

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:15.480
- Yeah, of course.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:16.313
- The question for David

00:46:16.313 --> 00:46:18.540
is about the community diagnostic centres.

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:21.150
Really great to see the numbers
of community diagnosed here,

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:22.320
it is already working.

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:24.311
Can you give a perspective
on the geographical spread

00:46:24.311 --> 00:46:26.220
and what the next stage is

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:28.223
in terms of going further with that?

00:46:28.223 --> 00:46:30.840
And the question for
Steve is that we are much

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.300
better equipped to treat COVID now,

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:36.240
and just a perspective from
Steve on the availability

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:38.670
of therapeutics and
effect that they're having

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:40.193
in terms of treating COVID.

00:46:41.190 --> 00:46:43.770
- So let's do all those questions,

00:46:43.770 --> 00:46:46.890
then Ruth I'll bring you
in for your comments.

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:48.319
So David, do you want to
pick up those questions?

00:46:48.319 --> 00:46:49.350
- Yeah, I'm very happy to do so,

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.300
and I'll begin by giving
my colleague, Tim Ferris,

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:53.010
a heads up that I'll be turning to him

00:46:53.010 --> 00:46:55.081
for the community diagnostic piece, Tim.

00:46:55.081 --> 00:46:58.436
In terms of the interventions,

00:46:58.436 --> 00:47:01.080
we have asked all systems to come forward,

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:04.680
to put their case about
how we do can best align

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:07.560
demand capacity into
the winter to make sure

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:10.680
that we do have the capacity
we need to meet that demand.

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:12.510
I think there'll be a variety of options.

00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:14.760
For some, it will be increases in beds.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:16.890
Some of those beds might
be hospital based beds.

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:18.683
Some might be community based beds.

00:47:18.683 --> 00:47:20.730
And also of course there are alternatives

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:22.416
in the creation of virtual wards,

00:47:22.416 --> 00:47:25.620
which some parts of the UK
have done extremely well.

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:27.450
And if we can generalise that at scale,

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.250
there are real, real opportunities there.

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:31.890
So I think there's a
range of capacity options

00:47:31.890 --> 00:47:34.820
that we've got that we're
working through at the moment.

00:47:34.820 --> 00:47:37.470
The other thing we need to
focus on is the ambulance

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:40.050
handovers at the front
door and making sure

00:47:40.050 --> 00:47:42.480
that we've got good
practises spread at scale,

00:47:42.480 --> 00:47:44.370
innovations in the right place.

00:47:44.370 --> 00:47:46.740
And again, there are
six systems that Pauline

00:47:46.740 --> 00:47:49.285
and Steve Powis are working
within great detail,

00:47:49.285 --> 00:47:53.280
which account for about
30% of the handover delays.

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.490
There's a variety and range of options

00:47:56.490 --> 00:47:58.320
that we're exploring
that we have available.

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:01.230
- It's unfair and you
may not want to comment.

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:03.930
Do you have a sense
for the potential scale

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:05.460
of the additional capacity,

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:07.977
be it multiple beds coming
physically available

00:48:07.977 --> 00:48:10.987
or virtual wards or other practises?

00:48:10.987 --> 00:48:12.540
- So, we're early...

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:14.880
Well, we're not--
- A sort of commitment.

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:15.713
- At the moment,

00:48:15.713 --> 00:48:17.280
I think if we looked at
what we've got though,

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:20.470
there is potential for
up to the equivalent of

00:48:20.470 --> 00:48:24.210
between 7 and 9,000 acute bed places,

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:27.030
but those would not necessarily
be acute beds per se.

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:28.980
So those would be beds.

00:48:28.980 --> 00:48:30.660
Some of those would be alternative beds

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:33.390
such as the virtual ward
options that we've talked about.

00:48:33.390 --> 00:48:35.130
But what I want to make absolutely sure

00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:38.100
that we kick the tyres on
these plans to make that we are

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:40.954
not either over optimistic
or under ambitious,

00:48:40.954 --> 00:48:42.464
getting that sweet spot right.

00:48:42.464 --> 00:48:43.413
- Absolutely.

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:47.610
Do you want to pick up the
community diagnostic centres?

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:50.130
- Sure, a great, great question.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:53.970
Just let me frame one
thing about what we mean

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:55.170
when we say diagnostics.

00:48:55.170 --> 00:48:57.420
So I think of them in four categories,

00:48:57.420 --> 00:49:00.840
blood tests, imaging tests, endoscopies,

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:04.170
and then physiologic testing,
which is actually more than,

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:08.670
it's a sort of a grab bag of
more than 50 different tests.

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:12.750
And very importantly,
so what is our position?

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:16.620
So our position on some
of those where we have

00:49:16.620 --> 00:49:20.580
the estate and the equipment
and the throughput,

00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:25.440
we are well over the 130%
of pre-pandemic levels.

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:28.020
On others, we are struggling to reach 90%.

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:29.760
So there's quite a variation

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:31.467
across all those different tests.

00:49:31.467 --> 00:49:34.380
And really it comes down to the specifics.

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:36.750
In terms of our approach right now...

00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:40.950
Oh, and I should say there's
an important overlap here.

00:49:40.950 --> 00:49:44.322
You talked about cancer
and the role of diagnostics

00:49:44.322 --> 00:49:47.670
in the waiting list issues.

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:51.390
So about 14% of the
people on the waiting list

00:49:51.390 --> 00:49:54.240
are waiting for diagnostic tests,

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.610
but there's again a
huge amount of variation

00:49:56.610 --> 00:49:58.410
across different tests.

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:02.460
The test with the
greatest number of people

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:06.180
on the waiting list is non
obstetric ultrasound for example,

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:09.360
cardiac echo being a close second.

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:12.510
So lots of variation.

00:50:12.510 --> 00:50:14.400
Let me just say, in our approach,

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:16.920
it's about capacity
throughput and innovation.

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:20.490
So on capacity, it's estate,
equipment and people.

00:50:20.490 --> 00:50:23.673
It turns out when you look very closely,

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:29.250
it is the people, the
operators of these equipment

00:50:29.250 --> 00:50:31.290
that is the biggest rate limiter

00:50:31.290 --> 00:50:34.320
down at the level of the
service being delivered.

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:37.890
And that is the largest source
of variation, the community.

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:40.260
So more directly to your question,

00:50:40.260 --> 00:50:42.360
the community diagnostic centres,

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:46.140
they have currently added
2% overall capacity averaged

00:50:46.140 --> 00:50:48.630
across all tests in all locations.

00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:51.673
The ambition is that
they will add up to 5%

00:50:51.673 --> 00:50:55.380
when they are completed in
approximately two years.

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:57.630
So we're adding capacity steadily.

00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:00.210
That is estate, equipment.

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:04.530
But again, the rate limiter
here actually is the people.

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:08.324
And they are specifically
planned for areas

00:51:08.324 --> 00:51:10.200
that have the greatest need

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:12.210
and that was a very conscious attempt

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:13.800
and that information is public.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:17.280
And I can make sure that
we can deliver on that.

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:20.820
And then finally, I just want
to say that the innovation

00:51:20.820 --> 00:51:24.090
actually plays an important role in this.

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:26.872
Very specifically, I said
there are two ultrasound tests

00:51:26.872 --> 00:51:31.872
that are leading the backlog
in terms of diagnostic tests.

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:36.270
It just so happens that
in the area of ultrasound,

00:51:36.270 --> 00:51:39.300
there is quite a bit of rapid innovation

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:40.950
using AI and other tools.

00:51:40.950 --> 00:51:45.210
And we are actively testing the
ability of those innovations

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:50.210
to be deployed more rapidly
and help us meet our ambitions

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:54.775
for making diagnostics available

00:51:54.775 --> 00:51:57.363
for everyone who needs
them when they need them.

00:51:58.260 --> 00:51:59.618
- Thank you, Tim.

00:51:59.618 --> 00:52:02.730
Julian, you want to
come in before being...

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:03.563
Do you want to make a comment?

00:52:03.563 --> 00:52:05.700
And in respect to David, my question.

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:08.520
- I just wanted to say, I mean,

00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:11.670
it's important when you hear
the kind of 7 to 9,000 number.

00:52:11.670 --> 00:52:14.130
I mean, that won't necessarily
translate specifically

00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:19.110
to an improvement of 7 to 9,000

00:52:19.110 --> 00:52:22.710
in our total occupancy number.

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:24.750
So I just think we need to target

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:28.686
the maximum we can possibly
do given the current context,

00:52:28.686 --> 00:52:32.703
but I doubt we will see
that 7 to 9% improvement

00:52:32.703 --> 00:52:34.710
in our occupancy.

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:37.350
I mean, as I said, that's
why David and the team

00:52:37.350 --> 00:52:39.660
are really testing what is doable

00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:41.580
and what impact you think it'll have.

00:52:41.580 --> 00:52:42.570
- No, I get that.

00:52:42.570 --> 00:52:44.370
Ruth, you had a question or comment.

00:52:45.210 --> 00:52:46.863
- Oh, sorry, yes, of course I do.

00:52:48.780 --> 00:52:49.890
And then Ruth.

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:51.240
- A question on therapeutics.

00:52:51.240 --> 00:52:55.560
So firstly, I always say
that it's quite remarkable

00:52:55.560 --> 00:52:57.750
in the pandemic how quickly both vaccines

00:52:57.750 --> 00:53:00.840
and a range of therapeutics
have been developed.

00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:02.910
And I think, you know, two
years, three months ago,

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:03.870
two years, four months ago,

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:08.220
I think we would've been
very surprised in advance

00:53:08.220 --> 00:53:09.570
to think that we would
be able to get these

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:11.400
into clinical practise so quickly,

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:13.470
so I think that's a real
triumph for science,

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:15.614
the clinical community, the regulators,

00:53:15.614 --> 00:53:17.910
and everybody in the health
systems who who've worked

00:53:17.910 --> 00:53:18.930
to deploy them.

00:53:18.930 --> 00:53:22.020
On therapeutics, COVID therapeutics,

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:25.726
so we stood up in December of last year

00:53:25.726 --> 00:53:28.743
in the out of hospital space,

00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:34.110
COVID medicine delivery
units to be able to deliver

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:36.840
both monoclonal antibodies,
which are given by injection,

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:39.990
but also oral medications,

00:53:39.990 --> 00:53:43.369
IE tablets to eligible,
potentially eligible

00:53:43.369 --> 00:53:47.670
members of the community
who develop COVID.

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:49.380
And those models are still in place

00:53:49.380 --> 00:53:52.712
and they're still working and
we are still delivering drugs

00:53:52.712 --> 00:53:56.400
to affected individuals as I speak.

00:53:56.400 --> 00:53:59.850
And clearly with a new wave,
we are doing more of that.

00:53:59.850 --> 00:54:03.873
The two main oral agents are
paxlovid and molnupiravir.

00:54:05.040 --> 00:54:07.980
We are targeting a population
of just over a million,

00:54:07.980 --> 00:54:10.290
a high risk population identified by

00:54:10.290 --> 00:54:13.380
an expert reference group,
expert clinical reference group.

00:54:13.380 --> 00:54:15.420
And those individuals have been contacted.

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:17.520
They have been given lateral flow tests

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:18.960
and we know when they test positive

00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:20.580
as long as they register
their positive tests,

00:54:20.580 --> 00:54:22.590
and they are proactively contacted

00:54:22.590 --> 00:54:24.900
by the COVID medicine delivery units.

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:26.340
A discussion is how to ensure

00:54:26.340 --> 00:54:27.810
that they are indeed eligible,

00:54:27.810 --> 00:54:31.379
that it's appropriate to
prescribe the therapeutic,

00:54:31.379 --> 00:54:32.670
choice of therapeutic.

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:34.587
And then the prescription is given

00:54:34.587 --> 00:54:36.523
and the therapeutics delivered.

00:54:36.523 --> 00:54:38.631
So that is working well.

00:54:38.631 --> 00:54:42.630
Monoclonal antibodies we are
still using in that system,

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:44.130
a slightly different approach.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:47.730
There is a question about
how we do that going forward.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:50.250
We've done this in development
with integrated care systems.

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:53.310
We are beginning to talk
to integrated care systems

00:54:53.310 --> 00:54:55.590
to our charity stakeholders who represent

00:54:55.590 --> 00:54:58.080
some of those high risk
patients and of course,

00:54:58.080 --> 00:54:59.700
teams in NHS England regionally

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:02.580
as to how we will deliver
that as we move into a more,

00:55:02.580 --> 00:55:05.460
if you want business as
usual management of COVID

00:55:05.460 --> 00:55:06.983
as an endemic disease.

00:55:06.983 --> 00:55:09.780
So that's a conversation we'll
be having over the autumn.

00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:11.040
And then the final thing I'd say

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:14.550
is there is a very important
trial called Panoramic.

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:17.010
Of course, one of the big
success stories in the UK

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:20.340
is how we've rapidly recruited
people into clinical trials.

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:21.660
initially in the hospital setting.

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:26.400
But Panoramic has been recruiting
in the community setting

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:28.500
through general practitioners
to look initially

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:32.097
at molnupiravir and
more likely at paxlovid

00:55:32.097 --> 00:55:34.647
in the population as a whole,

00:55:34.647 --> 00:55:39.420
not the high risk group that
we are already prescribing to.

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:41.310
And we are very hopeful that we'll get

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:43.980
the first set of results from paxlovid,

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:47.820
which will be with molnupiravir
in the next month or two.

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:51.020
And that will guide us
as to what any change

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:55.083
to that deployment strategy
should be in forthcoming months.

00:55:56.310 --> 00:55:57.693
- Thank you, very good.

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:00.063
- Ruth, I think you're on.

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:04.020
- Thank you, Chair, for
your patience on that.

00:56:04.020 --> 00:56:06.740
I didn't want to forget
children in all of this.

00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:07.950
So we've seen children and

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:11.790
young people's attendances and
admissions increase recently.

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:14.040
So together with Simon Kenny,

00:56:14.040 --> 00:56:16.143
we've restarted the work that we did

00:56:16.143 --> 00:56:19.680
like this time last year in
readiness to support children,

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:21.270
their admissions.

00:56:21.270 --> 00:56:22.860
And whilst we've got more nurses

00:56:22.860 --> 00:56:26.463
than we did have this time
last year and the year before,

00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:30.360
the pressures are real for
frontline staff, you know,

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:31.740
whether you've been to Liverpool,

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:35.790
or I was yesterday with
the Evelina Centre at GSTT.

00:56:35.790 --> 00:56:38.610
I'm enormously impressed and grateful

00:56:38.610 --> 00:56:41.760
for teams coming together
to support each other,

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:44.280
to make sure they're doing
those extra bank shifts,

00:56:44.280 --> 00:56:45.930
but the pressures are real.

00:56:45.930 --> 00:56:47.885
And that's why we've also got another

00:56:47.885 --> 00:56:52.440
international recruitment
offer out there at the moment.

00:56:52.440 --> 00:56:53.970
It closes tomorrow.

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:57.450
But that is making sure
that we support the recovery

00:56:57.450 --> 00:56:58.950
and support staffing in winter,

00:56:58.950 --> 00:57:03.950
'cos we are now up to over
42,000 international nurses

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:06.030
that we have welcomed into this country,

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:09.960
into our workforce in the
NHS since September '19.

00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:11.040
But the pressures are real.

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:12.540
But there's work that I know that

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:15.300
we are all grateful for people doing.

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:16.603
- Thank you.

00:57:16.603 --> 00:57:19.890
Can I just, Julian, bring you in briefly

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:23.790
for a financial update
first, the month through,

00:57:23.790 --> 00:57:26.910
and how you see the current
position and current pressures.

00:57:26.910 --> 00:57:28.050
- Sure.

00:57:28.050 --> 00:57:31.225
Look, since last time we met,

00:57:31.225 --> 00:57:35.266
we have had final plans back from systems

00:57:35.266 --> 00:57:39.960
as Amanda alluded to
in her opening remarks.

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.242
I mean, they've really
done an extraordinary job.

00:57:42.242 --> 00:57:44.160
Given all the pressures going on,

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:49.160
they've committed to
delivering financial plans.

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:51.780
That balance. There are
four or five systems

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:55.110
who are in the kind of
final plans they submitted

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:58.740
still had a gap of about 10 to 20 million,

00:57:58.740 --> 00:58:01.410
and they have committed to
continue to work with us

00:58:01.410 --> 00:58:03.390
to look at how that can be closed

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:05.580
over the course of the year.

00:58:05.580 --> 00:58:08.790
I mean, clearly there is
significant risk in those plans,

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:13.140
particularly when the original
spending review settlement

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:16.620
we were asked to plan on
the basis that COVID levels

00:58:16.620 --> 00:58:19.931
would be low and that we
would be able to take out

00:58:19.931 --> 00:58:22.530
about three billion pounds
of the exceptional costs

00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:24.000
we saw during COVID.

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:27.210
And clearly, as David just
said with COVID cases,

00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:30.243
COVID at 11,500 with
sickness absence rising.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:33.900
That is going to put pressure
for example on the agency,

00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:37.050
bill and cost of temporary
staffing to cover those gaps.

00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:41.370
So there really is significant
risk in those plans

00:58:41.370 --> 00:58:46.050
and where we'll just need to
keep working with colleagues

00:58:46.050 --> 00:58:48.807
on the front line to
properly assess where we are

00:58:48.807 --> 00:58:52.053
and make sensible decisions
with them through the year.

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:57.325
The other I would say significant issue

00:58:57.325 --> 00:59:02.325
also Amanda alluded to
it is the pay settlement.

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:06.810
Now we wait to see what
the pay review body

00:59:06.810 --> 00:59:08.010
actually recommend.

00:59:08.010 --> 00:59:12.090
As Amanda said, we were funded
for a maximum settlement

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:17.090
of 3%, clearly inflation running high.

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:20.490
I think at least some of the
papers covered what seemed

00:59:20.490 --> 00:59:23.343
likely to be the education
settlement a few days ago,

00:59:24.330 --> 00:59:26.640
which would clearly end up
with something significantly

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:30.030
north of the kind of 3%
number I just referred to.

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:35.030
Every percentage point that
you know, is over the 3% cost,

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:40.290
the NHS somewhere between
900 and a billion pounds,

00:59:40.290 --> 00:59:43.020
900 million and a billion pounds.

00:59:43.020 --> 00:59:46.230
So look, it's for the government

00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:49.088
to take the PRB recommendation
and decide what to do.

00:59:49.088 --> 00:59:52.140
We clearly are supportive
of the right settlement

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:55.230
for our staff in the current context,

00:59:55.230 --> 01:00:00.230
but absolutely think that
is a really important thing

01:00:01.620 --> 01:00:03.090
to deal with some of the
cost of living pressures

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.490
and the very real world
circumstances our staff are facing.

01:00:05.490 --> 01:00:09.600
Nonetheless, if it's higher than the 3%

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:12.300
and if we are living within the money

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:13.890
we would currently have,

01:00:13.890 --> 01:00:16.650
that would entail very
real decisions for us

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:17.610
about what we would stop.

01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:21.250
We are just at the point
where having had to fund

01:00:23.430 --> 01:00:24.420
a big inflationary pressure,

01:00:24.420 --> 01:00:26.400
I think we talked about
last time at the board,

01:00:26.400 --> 01:00:27.900
about one and a half billion pounds,

01:00:27.900 --> 01:00:32.900
and fund a cut to the
original NHS settlement

01:00:33.030 --> 01:00:34.770
in order to fund other pressures

01:00:34.770 --> 01:00:37.773
in the departmental position,
actually around COVID testing.

01:00:39.240 --> 01:00:44.130
You know, we have so to
speak, emptied the coffers.

01:00:44.130 --> 01:00:47.280
We've already had to reduce
the investment we are making

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:51.000
into some of our programmes
by about 800 million quid,

01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:53.400
800 million pounds already.

01:00:53.400 --> 01:00:57.820
So we would then be looking
at having to, I mean,

01:00:58.830 --> 01:01:01.440
cut back on investment in
our major areas, right?

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:04.440
When our major areas are
primary care, cancer care,

01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:06.360
or indeed at the margin, you know,

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:07.950
some big capital investments.

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:12.180
In fact, we were just talking
about the diagnostic centres.

01:01:12.180 --> 01:01:15.000
So look, we wait to see what
the government decision is.

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:19.200
We wait to see what the
government response would be,

01:01:19.200 --> 01:01:23.250
but as I said, a pay
settlement higher than 3%

01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:25.710
and no extra money would entail

01:01:25.710 --> 01:01:27.699
some really difficult decision.

01:01:27.699 --> 01:01:30.777
It is not realistic to ask systems quotes,

01:01:30.777 --> 01:01:32.580
to just do more efficiency in a world

01:01:32.580 --> 01:01:34.380
where we're already asking to do 4 to 5%.

01:01:34.380 --> 01:01:38.753
I mean, like 5.6 billion
pounds in cash terms.

01:01:38.753 --> 01:01:43.753
And in a world where total
NHS funding year on year,

01:01:43.811 --> 01:01:47.220
'21-'22 to '22-'23 is
actually real terms cut.

01:01:47.220 --> 01:01:48.330
I mean, to put it in cash terms,

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:51.810
it's a two and a half
billion pound real terms cut

01:01:51.810 --> 01:01:54.660
that we have already worked
out how we could live with,

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:57.810
which is the first real
terms cut in NHS funding,

01:01:57.810 --> 01:01:59.190
well, definitely since before '97.

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:01.950
I think it might even
be since the mid 1950s.

01:02:01.950 --> 01:02:06.270
So you know, it's a very
stretching position,

01:02:06.270 --> 01:02:11.070
but look, we wait to see what
the government decision is

01:02:11.070 --> 01:02:14.313
and we are clearly working with them.

01:02:15.540 --> 01:02:17.061
- Could I just have one--
- Please, yeah.

01:02:17.061 --> 01:02:20.340
- Just Julian's given
a really clear readout,

01:02:20.340 --> 01:02:22.080
but I think I just wanted to emphasise

01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:23.790
what Julian's written in his paper

01:02:23.790 --> 01:02:25.860
'cos I think it's really important

01:02:25.860 --> 01:02:28.890
to be absolutely clear
to build what he said.

01:02:28.890 --> 01:02:31.110
I mean, this is not just essential

01:02:31.110 --> 01:02:33.390
that the work of frontline NHS staff

01:02:33.390 --> 01:02:35.760
tackling the pandemic recovering services

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:38.100
is properly recognised and rewarded.

01:02:38.100 --> 01:02:41.430
It is also an operational necessity.

01:02:41.430 --> 01:02:44.490
So if we are to retain
the staff that we need

01:02:44.490 --> 01:02:46.710
to make further inroads into long waits

01:02:46.710 --> 01:02:48.930
and deal with all of the
operational pressures

01:02:48.930 --> 01:02:51.540
we've just discussed, then you know,

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:56.540
we have to be really clear that
the right pay for our staff

01:02:56.977 --> 01:03:01.113
is a critical component of that.

01:03:01.113 --> 01:03:03.300
- I'll then just add one
other thing just to pick up

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:07.770
David's description of what
we're doing on winter capacity.

01:03:07.770 --> 01:03:09.600
In the original planning
guidance we set out,

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:11.820
we put aside 250 million pounds

01:03:11.820 --> 01:03:14.550
for extra capacity through virtual wards.

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:16.977
Our assessment is we're going
to need to put more in than

01:03:16.977 --> 01:03:20.130
that, and even in the context
of everything I've just said,

01:03:20.130 --> 01:03:25.130
we are absolutely
prioritising that investment

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:29.099
because we think it is so
critical to restoring safe,

01:03:29.099 --> 01:03:32.190
you know, aiming to restore
some safe operating margin

01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:34.890
within our acute hospitals
as we look ahead to winter.

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:37.430
- Thank you Ruth.

01:03:37.430 --> 01:03:39.690
- Thank you Chairman and thank you Amanda

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:42.180
for being so explicit about your support

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:46.800
because the cost of living
is really affecting our staff

01:03:46.800 --> 01:03:51.120
of all backgrounds,
particularly community nurses

01:03:51.120 --> 01:03:52.680
with some of fuel costs.

01:03:52.680 --> 01:03:55.530
And so I'm grateful to those providers

01:03:55.530 --> 01:04:00.480
like Leeds Community, like
Cambridgeshire Community

01:04:00.480 --> 01:04:02.864
that have taken local actions

01:04:02.864 --> 01:04:05.910
to support their trust staff as well.

01:04:05.910 --> 01:04:08.610
So I'm pleased that people
are taking it, but yes,

01:04:08.610 --> 01:04:10.320
pay is a matter for ministers,

01:04:10.320 --> 01:04:12.120
but your support, Amanda, thank you.

01:04:16.230 --> 01:04:19.350
- I'm pausing, really, 'cos
actually it's a very sobering

01:04:19.350 --> 01:04:23.997
and necessarily sobering
picture, Julian that you present.

01:04:23.997 --> 01:04:26.944
Amanda, again, as Ruth's just said,

01:04:26.944 --> 01:04:29.070
I'm very grateful for
you calling that out,

01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:33.810
that message from Julian
in his update to the board.

01:04:33.810 --> 01:04:35.880
It is really difficult, this.

01:04:35.880 --> 01:04:38.850
I mean, inflation moving at these levels

01:04:38.850 --> 01:04:43.320
and the pressure that puts
on people, as we said,

01:04:43.320 --> 01:04:47.523
the cost of living, really,
really, really difficult.

01:04:49.290 --> 01:04:53.190
And these are circumstances
that many people

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:55.910
have either never had to deal with before,

01:04:55.910 --> 01:04:58.230
or have lost the ability to
think about how to deal with it.

01:04:58.230 --> 01:05:01.740
This step change actually in costs

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:04.773
because of inflation is
really, really tough.

01:05:06.210 --> 01:05:08.367
We have to protect the service

01:05:08.367 --> 01:05:10.740
and we have to protect our staff.

01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:12.240
We really must do that, actually.

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:15.120
And particularly, it's sort of a rather,

01:05:15.120 --> 01:05:16.980
it's going to sound really
glib and I apologise,

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:19.527
but actually when you see
the cost of energy moving up

01:05:19.527 --> 01:05:20.937
and the cost of fuel moving up

01:05:20.937 --> 01:05:23.630
and the cost of food
stuffs moving up and so on

01:05:23.630 --> 01:05:25.740
at the rate that we are seeing,

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:29.550
a number of people can absorb it,

01:05:29.550 --> 01:05:34.170
but actually majority of our
staff are not in that level.

01:05:34.170 --> 01:05:37.050
They're not paid at the
amounts where they can actually

01:05:37.050 --> 01:05:40.271
absorb these degrees of
increase in their fuel costs

01:05:40.271 --> 01:05:42.810
or energy more broadly.

01:05:42.810 --> 01:05:45.660
And we have to make sure that
we are looking after them.

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:47.007
We really do.

01:05:47.007 --> 01:05:48.540
And I won't give the statistic,

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:51.960
but actually I was upset,
really upset actually,

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:54.480
in terms of the message I've been given

01:05:54.480 --> 01:05:57.210
on a couple of my visits around, that

01:05:57.210 --> 01:05:59.460
numbers of our staff
who are employed by us

01:05:59.460 --> 01:06:01.020
who are going to food banks.

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:05.910
So this is really, really
difficult, really difficult.

01:06:05.910 --> 01:06:06.827
So Jeremy, do you want to--

01:06:06.827 --> 01:06:09.240
- Just a quick point,
it may sound obvious,

01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:12.030
but obviously if we can
drive some efficiencies

01:06:12.030 --> 01:06:15.300
and effectiveness and help fund this,

01:06:15.300 --> 01:06:16.680
that'd be really useful,

01:06:16.680 --> 01:06:18.030
but I'd also like to make the point

01:06:18.030 --> 01:06:19.950
that we've got to be very careful

01:06:19.950 --> 01:06:21.720
in trying to manage our budgets.

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:23.040
We don't cut the investment.

01:06:23.040 --> 01:06:27.060
We need to make the system to
drive the efficiencies that

01:06:27.060 --> 01:06:28.971
will make better care affordable

01:06:28.971 --> 01:06:29.804
and paid for.
- Absolutely.

01:06:29.804 --> 01:06:30.840
- So I just want to make that...

01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:33.120
I know it sounds obvious perhaps,

01:06:33.120 --> 01:06:35.850
but we've got to be careful not
trying to manage to a number

01:06:35.850 --> 01:06:38.460
that we mortgaged the future
in terms of reducing--

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:42.240
- It's why this is a really
different type of challenge

01:06:42.240 --> 01:06:44.610
that many managements have
now having to deal with

01:06:44.610 --> 01:06:46.170
in many organisations and industries.

01:06:46.170 --> 01:06:48.150
Actually, it's a really tough space.

01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:48.983
- Well, I was going to say though,

01:06:48.983 --> 01:06:50.160
just to pick up in Jeremy's point,

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:52.920
I think that is why for
me two things, one again,

01:06:52.920 --> 01:06:54.800
the work that you are
going to lead on efficiency

01:06:54.800 --> 01:06:57.960
is so important, given that
there is still a gap to find

01:06:57.960 --> 01:06:59.747
before we talk about any additional

01:06:59.747 --> 01:07:02.850
over or above the current
very stretching challenge

01:07:02.850 --> 01:07:06.330
NHS has accepted and rightly so.

01:07:06.330 --> 01:07:08.970
But secondly, why I think
it is also so important

01:07:08.970 --> 01:07:10.890
to Julian's point that we
give really clear advice

01:07:10.890 --> 01:07:12.300
to government on this,

01:07:12.300 --> 01:07:14.854
because ultimately this is where I think

01:07:14.854 --> 01:07:17.328
we have to do our duty to be clear

01:07:17.328 --> 01:07:20.073
about implications and consequences.

01:07:21.660 --> 01:07:23.070
- I'm slightly conscious of time.

01:07:23.070 --> 01:07:25.920
Can I move us on?

01:07:25.920 --> 01:07:27.659
And actually it's quite a good intro,

01:07:27.659 --> 01:07:30.570
Jacqui, to you I think.

01:07:30.570 --> 01:07:34.620
So you're billed here as
the new commercial strategy

01:07:34.620 --> 01:07:37.860
for the NHS is the title I
think of the agenda item.

01:07:37.860 --> 01:07:39.660
- Thank you very much.
- And thank you for the paper,

01:07:39.660 --> 01:07:40.493
but would you actually give it...

01:07:40.493 --> 01:07:42.270
I'm assuming the paper's been read,

01:07:42.270 --> 01:07:44.550
and would you draw out
the key points for us?

01:07:44.550 --> 01:07:45.383
- Absolutely.

01:07:45.383 --> 01:07:47.340
Thank you for the opportunity
to bring the paper to alive.

01:07:47.340 --> 01:07:50.675
So just a few sort of
key facts commercially.

01:07:50.675 --> 01:07:52.740
So the NHS is one of the biggest

01:07:52.740 --> 01:07:55.650
commercial organisations in the country,

01:07:55.650 --> 01:07:58.740
buying on behalf of the fifth
biggest employer in the world.

01:07:58.740 --> 01:08:00.390
And we have, by far,

01:08:00.390 --> 01:08:04.110
the highest commercial
spend of public money.

01:08:04.110 --> 01:08:07.200
Our clinicians rely on
our NHS commercial teams

01:08:07.200 --> 01:08:09.420
to buy the goods and services they need,

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:11.700
to be at the right
place at the right time.

01:08:11.700 --> 01:08:14.580
So it's absolutely the
function that we do is critical

01:08:14.580 --> 01:08:17.040
to the delivery of patient care.

01:08:17.040 --> 01:08:20.790
This scale of the NHS commercial
activity is significant.

01:08:20.790 --> 01:08:24.300
We've got over 35 billion
of addressable spend

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:28.500
that is spent commercially
across over 80,000 suppliers

01:08:28.500 --> 01:08:30.450
and across multiple categories.

01:08:30.450 --> 01:08:33.930
And we've actually got over
4,000 procurement professionals

01:08:33.930 --> 01:08:37.290
doing that buying in
various commercial teams

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:39.870
at national, regional, local levels,

01:08:39.870 --> 01:08:41.430
along with supporting partners,

01:08:41.430 --> 01:08:44.100
such as the NHS Supply Chain.

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:47.310
So this is an incredibly
complex environment

01:08:47.310 --> 01:08:49.980
and our commercial procurement functions

01:08:49.980 --> 01:08:51.961
do play a central role in ensuring

01:08:51.961 --> 01:08:54.930
the financial stability of the NHS,

01:08:54.930 --> 01:08:58.560
but there are significant
challenges with the current way

01:08:58.560 --> 01:09:02.550
we have been executing
commercial capability in the NHS.

01:09:02.550 --> 01:09:05.640
At present, there's
currently no centrally driven

01:09:05.640 --> 01:09:07.860
NHS commercial strategy that binds

01:09:07.860 --> 01:09:11.520
all of these NHS commercial
communities together,

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:14.490
that drives collaboration
across commercial teams,

01:09:14.490 --> 01:09:17.520
that leverages that
collective buying power

01:09:17.520 --> 01:09:21.630
or provides a clear, consistent
guideline on how to contract

01:09:21.630 --> 01:09:24.900
and work from a supply
perspective with the NHS.

01:09:24.900 --> 01:09:27.600
So this has resulted in
unwarranted variation

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:30.600
in commercial arrangements with suppliers,

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:33.270
missed opportunities to
drive additional value,

01:09:33.270 --> 01:09:36.420
leverage scale, and harness
collective buying power,

01:09:36.420 --> 01:09:39.540
and really importantly,
lead and shape the market,

01:09:39.540 --> 01:09:41.160
which is exactly what we should be doing

01:09:41.160 --> 01:09:43.980
with the size and the scale of what we do.

01:09:43.980 --> 01:09:46.147
So with this lack of central direction

01:09:46.147 --> 01:09:49.383
to provide clear buying
pathways, routes to market,

01:09:50.940 --> 01:09:53.490
I'll call it out, poorly
integrated systems

01:09:53.490 --> 01:09:56.880
and data and technology,
a common issue we have,

01:09:56.880 --> 01:10:01.020
but absolutely one very clear
in the commercial field.

01:10:01.020 --> 01:10:02.820
It's been very, very difficult just for us

01:10:02.820 --> 01:10:04.800
to really be able to do that modelling

01:10:04.800 --> 01:10:06.960
and analytics on the spend.

01:10:06.960 --> 01:10:09.360
The lack of strategies,
commercial strategies,

01:10:09.360 --> 01:10:12.630
category strategies means
that we are buying things

01:10:12.630 --> 01:10:17.630
in many, many different ways
across the entire environment

01:10:18.030 --> 01:10:20.850
and our supplier relationships
are quite fragmented.

01:10:20.850 --> 01:10:22.230
So this has been really difficult.

01:10:22.230 --> 01:10:25.260
The suppliers are telling
me for them to work with us,

01:10:25.260 --> 01:10:27.000
to deliver the best contract,

01:10:27.000 --> 01:10:30.120
to deliver the best service
and more importantly,

01:10:30.120 --> 01:10:33.510
to be really in innovative
with us in the NHS

01:10:33.510 --> 01:10:35.735
to deliver to our priorities.

01:10:35.735 --> 01:10:37.770
This has also meant that with the lack

01:10:37.770 --> 01:10:39.420
of supplier management that we've got

01:10:39.420 --> 01:10:42.210
across the organisation
at the moment, you know,

01:10:42.210 --> 01:10:43.980
I've been sort of looking at the numbers

01:10:43.980 --> 01:10:46.800
and we've got quite a lot of
our key strategic suppliers

01:10:46.800 --> 01:10:49.830
that have got in excess of
a hundred contracts with us

01:10:49.830 --> 01:10:52.560
for the same product over a hundred times

01:10:52.560 --> 01:10:55.890
at different pricing and different
service levels agreement.

01:10:55.890 --> 01:10:59.130
This just does not make any
commercial sense at all.

01:10:59.130 --> 01:11:01.470
So what am I going to do about it?

01:11:01.470 --> 01:11:06.180
So this is the recommendation
and what we are building.

01:11:06.180 --> 01:11:08.280
NHS England is now pulling together

01:11:08.280 --> 01:11:11.100
and building a central
commercial function.

01:11:11.100 --> 01:11:13.980
This is going to be a new
dedicated central chain

01:11:13.980 --> 01:11:17.310
that is going to be
run out of NHS England.

01:11:17.310 --> 01:11:21.450
And the ambition is really
very much co-creation,

01:11:21.450 --> 01:11:26.100
collaboration and to unify
that NHS commercial community

01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:28.135
so we can work on the
things and the issues

01:11:28.135 --> 01:11:30.720
that I've just told you to
unlock those opportunities

01:11:30.720 --> 01:11:34.110
for the NHS and by the
level that delivers value

01:11:34.110 --> 01:11:36.510
for every pound spent.

01:11:36.510 --> 01:11:38.130
Really important to say here,

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:42.390
this is a centre led initiative,
not a centralization.

01:11:42.390 --> 01:11:43.890
I want to be very clear about that

01:11:43.890 --> 01:11:45.540
because it's very, very different.

01:11:45.540 --> 01:11:47.731
So what the central commercial function

01:11:47.731 --> 01:11:48.564
is going to be doing,

01:11:48.564 --> 01:11:52.110
it's going to be offering
seven new service offerings.

01:11:52.110 --> 01:11:54.360
I'll just give you some of
the highlights of those.

01:11:54.360 --> 01:11:56.723
And we are going to be
doing work around commercial

01:11:56.723 --> 01:12:00.240
capability, putting in place
technical routes to market.

01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:05.240
So we make sure that everybody
creates single buying portals

01:12:05.790 --> 01:12:09.060
and we buy in the same consistent way.

01:12:09.060 --> 01:12:10.770
For our people and community,

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:12.930
we are going to be
creating a collaborative,

01:12:12.930 --> 01:12:15.570
connected culture across the NHS

01:12:15.570 --> 01:12:18.180
and ensure we're going to
professionalise this workforce

01:12:18.180 --> 01:12:21.000
so that we've got
qualified accredited teams,

01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:24.000
which will be supported
by career pathways.

01:12:24.000 --> 01:12:26.010
We're going to be focusing our governance,

01:12:26.010 --> 01:12:27.447
assurance and process.

01:12:27.447 --> 01:12:30.060
The NHS England, we already do this job,

01:12:30.060 --> 01:12:32.700
but let's make sure that
we endorse a standardised

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:35.340
and compliant approach within the market.

01:12:35.340 --> 01:12:37.110
On our technology and data,

01:12:37.110 --> 01:12:39.210
we are going to be rolling out technology

01:12:39.210 --> 01:12:41.730
to provide greater visibility of spend.

01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:43.620
And everybody has signed up to this.

01:12:43.620 --> 01:12:45.930
The collaboration has been phenomenal.

01:12:45.930 --> 01:12:48.450
I haven't actually come
across a single Chief Exec,

01:12:48.450 --> 01:12:50.040
a single commercial leader,

01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:53.850
a single CFO who said this
isn't what we should be doing.

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:55.650
So everybody signed up for this.

01:12:55.650 --> 01:12:59.657
And for the first time, the
first time in NHS history,

01:12:59.657 --> 01:13:01.740
at the end of the financial year,

01:13:01.740 --> 01:13:04.980
we'll have oversight of all
the commercial activities

01:13:04.980 --> 01:13:07.380
because we're going to be
using a common platform,

01:13:07.380 --> 01:13:10.590
enabling us, the NHS, to
world greater influence

01:13:10.590 --> 01:13:13.800
and over the market and
therefore promote innovation.

01:13:13.800 --> 01:13:17.460
So that's going to be really,
really key on technology.

01:13:17.460 --> 01:13:20.060
We're going to be putting in
place category strategies.

01:13:21.240 --> 01:13:23.700
You would think we already had these.

01:13:23.700 --> 01:13:25.680
We have, but more at local level,

01:13:25.680 --> 01:13:29.130
not at this sort of the
clinical pathway level

01:13:29.130 --> 01:13:32.100
and at the products
and the services level.

01:13:32.100 --> 01:13:34.467
And you know, we've been
working on a couple of these

01:13:34.467 --> 01:13:36.180
and just some examples.

01:13:36.180 --> 01:13:38.460
We looked at the cardio pathway

01:13:38.460 --> 01:13:42.360
and we sat down with senior clinicians.

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:44.700
And we looked at just
how we were buying things

01:13:44.700 --> 01:13:46.350
at every single stage of that

01:13:46.350 --> 01:13:48.930
to put a commercial strategy in place.

01:13:48.930 --> 01:13:51.180
And the result of that
was quite phenomenal.

01:13:51.180 --> 01:13:53.670
It was an immediate 40 million savings

01:13:53.670 --> 01:13:57.120
because we reduced
variation, we reduced waste.

01:13:57.120 --> 01:14:02.100
We managed to mitigate supply chain risk

01:14:02.100 --> 01:14:04.890
and supply chain availability.

01:14:04.890 --> 01:14:07.470
So when you have a
collective category in place,

01:14:07.470 --> 01:14:09.300
it makes a significant difference.

01:14:09.300 --> 01:14:12.000
So we are going to be continuing
to put these categories

01:14:12.000 --> 01:14:15.000
in place across all our
products and services

01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:16.770
across the NHS.

01:14:16.770 --> 01:14:18.120
Another one of our service offerings,

01:14:18.120 --> 01:14:19.590
we're going to continue to drive

01:14:19.590 --> 01:14:22.500
supply sustainability and innovation,

01:14:22.500 --> 01:14:24.480
where we've already been
leading the net zero

01:14:24.480 --> 01:14:28.200
and the social value side of the work

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:30.360
and working with industry partners, again,

01:14:30.360 --> 01:14:33.600
to identify those innovative solutions

01:14:33.600 --> 01:14:35.970
that we can actually scale up and share.

01:14:35.970 --> 01:14:37.140
And that's what's really key here.

01:14:37.140 --> 01:14:38.700
It's about the sharing.

01:14:38.700 --> 01:14:40.500
Whereas I've been going
around to the various trusts,

01:14:40.500 --> 01:14:44.253
I see amazing commercial
activity being undertaken,

01:14:44.253 --> 01:14:46.860
amazing, amazing teams, but you know,

01:14:46.860 --> 01:14:49.080
in terms of how do we then scale that up,

01:14:49.080 --> 01:14:51.000
and we all leverage that across the NHS.

01:14:51.000 --> 01:14:53.400
So there'll be a big piece
of work focusing on that.

01:14:53.400 --> 01:14:55.680
And finally on our service offerings,

01:14:55.680 --> 01:14:57.570
sourcing and management.

01:14:57.570 --> 01:15:00.870
So, you know, we spend public money,

01:15:00.870 --> 01:15:02.400
we are guardians of public money.

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:05.490
Therefore, we have to make sure that we do

01:15:05.490 --> 01:15:07.590
really adequate contract management,

01:15:07.590 --> 01:15:09.567
really adequate supplier management.

01:15:09.567 --> 01:15:11.400
And so this is something that the central

01:15:11.400 --> 01:15:13.140
commercial function will be rolling out

01:15:13.140 --> 01:15:15.390
the best practise across the NHS

01:15:15.390 --> 01:15:17.940
to ensure that we actually
are enforcing that

01:15:17.940 --> 01:15:20.880
and delivering to ensure value for money.

01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:23.850
And also finally, just
ensuring that we put in place

01:15:23.850 --> 01:15:26.460
a strategic supplier programme.

01:15:26.460 --> 01:15:28.560
Our suppliers want it, we want it.

01:15:28.560 --> 01:15:31.770
So we can actually sit down
with our top strategic suppliers

01:15:31.770 --> 01:15:35.313
and work out how they can
best engage with the NHS.

01:15:36.240 --> 01:15:41.240
So the idea is that we are
going to be rolling this out.

01:15:41.730 --> 01:15:45.300
We've already got the initial
capability out in place,

01:15:45.300 --> 01:15:49.380
and we will finish a lot of
those tasks by the end of 2022.

01:15:49.380 --> 01:15:51.470
I wish to have full operating capability

01:15:51.470 --> 01:15:54.867
in place by December, 2024.

01:15:54.867 --> 01:15:57.810
And at this point, the
central commercial function

01:15:57.810 --> 01:16:01.110
will effectively be
providing a range of services

01:16:01.110 --> 01:16:03.750
covering the entire commercial life cycle

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:07.650
and providing national,
regional and local benefits.

01:16:07.650 --> 01:16:08.483
So thank you very much.

01:16:08.483 --> 01:16:12.177
Today, I'm asking the
board to endorse the paper

01:16:12.177 --> 01:16:14.730
and support us in what we are doing,

01:16:14.730 --> 01:16:18.870
and specifically champion
the commercial activity

01:16:18.870 --> 01:16:21.390
at the integrated care system level.

01:16:21.390 --> 01:16:24.360
It's absolutely critical
as we go through with each

01:16:24.360 --> 01:16:27.696
of the ICSs and we build their
commercial strategies out

01:16:27.696 --> 01:16:30.360
that they are on board with this,

01:16:30.360 --> 01:16:32.580
and we start buying in
a collective manner.

01:16:32.580 --> 01:16:33.780
Thank you very much.

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:34.620
- Thank you, Jacqui.

01:16:34.620 --> 01:16:36.960
Any comments or questions?

01:16:36.960 --> 01:16:37.793
Mike, yeah.

01:16:37.793 --> 01:16:40.140
And then Susan and then Julian.

01:16:40.140 --> 01:16:42.090
- Just to support the work
that you've been doing,

01:16:42.090 --> 01:16:43.950
and it's great to see
it all coming together.

01:16:43.950 --> 01:16:45.850
I think you've made a huge difference.

01:16:46.740 --> 01:16:47.820
For the cynics amongst us,

01:16:47.820 --> 01:16:49.290
we might take the view that our suppliers

01:16:49.290 --> 01:16:51.840
have been playing off the
sides against the middle.

01:16:51.840 --> 01:16:53.274
What's your view,

01:16:53.274 --> 01:16:54.200
or it would be helpful to hear your view

01:16:54.200 --> 01:16:57.390
on what you might expect from
our suppliers looking forward

01:16:57.390 --> 01:17:00.390
in some of the challenges
you'd put back to them

01:17:00.390 --> 01:17:02.700
in the future of bringing
together a number of the contracts

01:17:02.700 --> 01:17:03.780
that you're talking about.

01:17:03.780 --> 01:17:07.560
By definition, whatever we
win, they may well lose,

01:17:07.560 --> 01:17:09.870
or at least we got to find a
way of cooperating with them

01:17:09.870 --> 01:17:12.102
to make sure that both sides of the table

01:17:12.102 --> 01:17:14.250
are actually getting something
out of the relationships.

01:17:14.250 --> 01:17:17.730
- Absolutely, and yeah, I've
had extensive conversations

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:20.910
with not only our really
large strategic suppliers,

01:17:20.910 --> 01:17:24.810
there's always that
sort of 80, 20, the 20%.

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:26.640
But also with our small SMEs as well,

01:17:26.640 --> 01:17:29.520
to ensure they can carry
on doing work with us

01:17:29.520 --> 01:17:31.140
'cos often a lot of the innovation

01:17:31.140 --> 01:17:34.620
will come out of the small suppliers.

01:17:34.620 --> 01:17:39.620
And yeah, it's interesting,
when you show the portfolio

01:17:39.723 --> 01:17:42.630
to a Chief Exec of one
of our major suppliers

01:17:42.630 --> 01:17:45.930
in whichever category, and
the amount of contracts

01:17:45.930 --> 01:17:47.790
that they're currently running, you know,

01:17:47.790 --> 01:17:49.470
I sort of make the comment,

01:17:49.470 --> 01:17:51.200
it must be like the old saying,

01:17:51.200 --> 01:17:53.820
painting the fourth
bridge for them as well.

01:17:53.820 --> 01:17:56.640
You know, they must be constantly pitching

01:17:56.640 --> 01:17:58.260
our time and time again.

01:17:58.260 --> 01:18:00.450
Well, they are constantly
pitching time and time again.

01:18:00.450 --> 01:18:01.283
It makes note.

01:18:01.283 --> 01:18:04.560
So they're very, very keen
to actually work together,

01:18:04.560 --> 01:18:06.810
especially when it's a
common good and service.

01:18:06.810 --> 01:18:09.240
And some of those categories
we're going to go after first

01:18:09.240 --> 01:18:11.340
and straight off, take for example,

01:18:11.340 --> 01:18:14.520
a hard and a soft FM, a
really obvious category.

01:18:14.520 --> 01:18:17.790
We don't want to be buying it 5200 times.

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:18.623
We can come together.

01:18:18.623 --> 01:18:23.623
So they actually are very
happy to come to the table.

01:18:23.703 --> 01:18:25.353
They understand of course,

01:18:25.353 --> 01:18:29.040
that that will change the
landscape of the procurement.

01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:31.710
So at the moment, you know,
there's lots of opportunities.

01:18:31.710 --> 01:18:33.300
If you're going to buy it 200 times,

01:18:33.300 --> 01:18:34.890
there's lots of pieces for you to get.

01:18:34.890 --> 01:18:37.260
Whereas actually if I'm
going to be consolidating up,

01:18:37.260 --> 01:18:42.260
but actually all I hear from
our suppliers is that desire

01:18:42.840 --> 01:18:46.530
to do the right thing by the
NHS and for them to be able

01:18:46.530 --> 01:18:47.910
to work with us in a much more complex.

01:18:47.910 --> 01:18:49.770
- So Jacqui, and what I'm going to do,

01:18:49.770 --> 01:18:52.410
you may need to make a note
'cos there's lots of people

01:18:52.410 --> 01:18:54.090
waving at me to ask questions.

01:18:54.090 --> 01:18:56.880
So I'm going to get all the
questions gathered for you.

01:18:56.880 --> 01:18:58.710
So probably make a note
of them as we go around,

01:18:58.710 --> 01:18:59.880
then answer them.

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:02.746
So we're going to go Susan then Simon,

01:19:02.746 --> 01:19:05.619
then Tim, then Julian.

01:19:05.619 --> 01:19:08.160
- Yeah, maybe mine is
not as much a question

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:10.980
as first of all, I
wholeheartedly endorse this.

01:19:10.980 --> 01:19:12.930
I think what you're
doing is really terrific.

01:19:12.930 --> 01:19:15.090
But just a comment, you know,

01:19:15.090 --> 01:19:17.596
this is something that big organisations,

01:19:17.596 --> 01:19:20.430
there's always a constant push
and pull as they, you know,

01:19:20.430 --> 01:19:22.860
what goes to the centre,
what gets pushed down,

01:19:22.860 --> 01:19:24.630
how the balance works.

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:28.950
And just more an offer of
help from and probably others

01:19:28.950 --> 01:19:30.753
of my colleagues around the table.

01:19:31.860 --> 01:19:33.900
There are a lot of people
who have been a part

01:19:33.900 --> 01:19:35.490
of these sort of supply chain efforts.

01:19:35.490 --> 01:19:37.440
And if it can be helpful to you,

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:39.630
I can make some introductions to others

01:19:39.630 --> 01:19:42.630
from big organisations where
we've had this push and pull.

01:19:42.630 --> 01:19:44.010
So really, just--
- Thank you.

01:19:44.010 --> 01:19:45.510
- Just let me know if there's a--

01:19:45.510 --> 01:19:46.445
- Thank you very much.

01:19:46.445 --> 01:19:48.240
- Thank you, Simon?

01:19:48.240 --> 01:19:49.460
- Thank you Chair.

01:19:49.460 --> 01:19:51.420
So Jacqui, a huge supporter
of the initiative.

01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:53.010
I think it's exactly
the right thing to do.

01:19:53.010 --> 01:19:55.305
Around technology,
there's huge opportunity.

01:19:55.305 --> 01:19:58.230
The Microsoft deal we
did a couple of years ago

01:19:58.230 --> 01:20:01.470
I think is a great example
of where we really delivered

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:05.040
additional capability
at a much reduced cost.

01:20:05.040 --> 01:20:08.233
My question is with those
big technology deals,

01:20:08.233 --> 01:20:11.160
kind of enterprise style deals,

01:20:11.160 --> 01:20:14.100
there's a likelihood that the
supplier is going to want to

01:20:14.100 --> 01:20:19.100
move the cost from the edge,
the providers, to the centre.

01:20:19.790 --> 01:20:22.347
So have you got a view as to how we move

01:20:22.347 --> 01:20:25.170
and how we pay for those
deals to the centre

01:20:25.170 --> 01:20:28.590
while still seeing the
benefit in the providers?

01:20:28.590 --> 01:20:30.990
- So Jacqui, hold for the moment.

01:20:30.990 --> 01:20:32.831
Remember that question.

01:20:32.831 --> 01:20:34.980
And, by the way,

01:20:34.980 --> 01:20:36.810
there's a reason you are
sitting next to Jeremy,

01:20:36.810 --> 01:20:38.940
by the way...

01:20:38.940 --> 01:20:39.900
Tim, you have a?

01:20:39.900 --> 01:20:41.295
- Actually, I'll see,

01:20:41.295 --> 01:20:44.940
because that was precisely my question.

01:20:44.940 --> 01:20:46.800
- Very good, which just
then leaves Julian,

01:20:46.800 --> 01:20:48.720
unless that was also your question.

01:20:48.720 --> 01:20:52.350
- Well, I was going to say
just a couple of things.

01:20:52.350 --> 01:20:54.780
First of all, I mean look,
it's quite interesting

01:20:54.780 --> 01:20:56.629
as we were talking
about sort of delegation

01:20:56.629 --> 01:20:59.407
and what you do centrally.

01:20:59.407 --> 01:21:02.370
And I think this is an
example where we will benefit

01:21:02.370 --> 01:21:04.500
from standardisation and
indeed the government

01:21:04.500 --> 01:21:07.290
published a sort of
data strategy only what,

01:21:07.290 --> 01:21:09.780
like two weeks ago, which again,

01:21:09.780 --> 01:21:12.330
has some of the same
flavour of where is it right

01:21:12.330 --> 01:21:15.390
to set some standards,
get benefits of scale,

01:21:15.390 --> 01:21:16.470
and not reinvent the wheel.

01:21:16.470 --> 01:21:18.561
And I think this is one of those areas.

01:21:18.561 --> 01:21:20.580
The second thing is actually,

01:21:20.580 --> 01:21:22.320
let's just recognise the
work we're building on.

01:21:22.320 --> 01:21:25.963
You know, this isn't just kind
of NHS is wasting of money.

01:21:25.963 --> 01:21:28.522
You know, there's been a
huge amount of work over

01:21:28.522 --> 01:21:30.147
recent years to set up CCL.

01:21:30.147 --> 01:21:32.070
We've done the sorts of things that Simon

01:21:32.070 --> 01:21:33.240
has just described.

01:21:33.240 --> 01:21:35.460
Actually, I think we are
world leading in our approach

01:21:35.460 --> 01:21:40.454
on medicines where we
get extraordinary value

01:21:40.454 --> 01:21:44.733
on behalf of both patients
and taxpayers in this country.

01:21:45.570 --> 01:21:47.460
And I think we just
need to recognise this.

01:21:47.460 --> 01:21:49.290
This is like the next step of a journey

01:21:49.290 --> 01:21:52.983
of just continual improvement.

01:21:54.060 --> 01:21:58.950
And I think it's quite
interesting, Mike's question,

01:21:58.950 --> 01:22:00.990
because I think again, there
are things we can learn

01:22:00.990 --> 01:22:02.220
from what we've done on medicines,

01:22:02.220 --> 01:22:04.590
which isn't just how
do we drive price down,

01:22:04.590 --> 01:22:06.690
as we've done an extraordinary
amount over recent years

01:22:06.690 --> 01:22:10.440
to look at how we accelerate
bringing things through,

01:22:10.440 --> 01:22:12.540
supporting people to drive innovation,

01:22:12.540 --> 01:22:13.890
linking it to the research.

01:22:13.890 --> 01:22:15.930
I mean, I'll flag what we've
done on the cancer drugs fund.

01:22:15.930 --> 01:22:18.390
I'll flag the innovative
medicines framework,

01:22:18.390 --> 01:22:20.580
the managed access agreements
we have put in place,

01:22:20.580 --> 01:22:23.994
the way actually we're
working with the MHRA and NICE

01:22:23.994 --> 01:22:27.360
to think actually, what is in innovative?

01:22:27.360 --> 01:22:29.670
How can we agree commercial deals

01:22:29.670 --> 01:22:32.700
that allow upfront investment
by the private sector,

01:22:32.700 --> 01:22:34.710
which it delivers certain goals.

01:22:34.710 --> 01:22:35.670
You know, there is a benefit.

01:22:35.670 --> 01:22:38.280
And I think again, how
do we learn those lessons

01:22:38.280 --> 01:22:41.430
as we look at other
areas and in particular,

01:22:41.430 --> 01:22:44.430
the work that Jacqui's flagged
around medical devices.

01:22:44.430 --> 01:22:47.820
Because unlike, let's say
drugs, where prices go down,

01:22:47.820 --> 01:22:49.770
it's interesting how the
price of medical devices

01:22:49.770 --> 01:22:51.480
hasn't really changed at all

01:22:51.480 --> 01:22:54.780
if you look at the last 20 years.

01:22:54.780 --> 01:22:58.080
But if we can work correctly
with those organisations,

01:22:58.080 --> 01:22:59.010
you can then look at how can we

01:22:59.010 --> 01:23:00.990
pull through innovation faster.

01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:04.020
So you know, it's value for taxpayer,

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:05.060
it's value for patients.

01:23:05.060 --> 01:23:08.001
You know, if we can keep
that in mind, we'll succeed.

01:23:08.001 --> 01:23:09.224
- Thank you, Julian.

01:23:09.224 --> 01:23:11.010
Now Jacqui--
- I have one.

01:23:11.010 --> 01:23:12.870
- Well, we're going to
have Andrew then Jeremy,

01:23:12.870 --> 01:23:14.970
they will be the last two.

01:23:14.970 --> 01:23:15.803
And then Jacqui,

01:23:15.803 --> 01:23:18.032
you can answer the questions
that have been put.

01:23:18.032 --> 01:23:18.870
So Andrew.

01:23:18.870 --> 01:23:20.730
- So I think one of the
biggest challenges we've got

01:23:20.730 --> 01:23:23.730
is around getting clinicians
on side in all of this.

01:23:23.730 --> 01:23:28.730
So a typical trust, we'll
spend 10% of its turnover

01:23:28.830 --> 01:23:31.560
on medical and surgical consumables.

01:23:31.560 --> 01:23:33.148
So it'd be good to understand

01:23:33.148 --> 01:23:36.120
how we're going to kind
of take up that challenge.

01:23:36.120 --> 01:23:37.980
'Cos if we can get the clinicians

01:23:37.980 --> 01:23:40.230
helping us to make the right decisions,

01:23:40.230 --> 01:23:42.720
I think that's where there's
some really big opportunities

01:23:42.720 --> 01:23:44.943
to make some significant savings.

01:23:45.780 --> 01:23:46.613
- Jeremy.

01:23:46.613 --> 01:23:48.120
- Very quickly, just
interested how you think

01:23:48.120 --> 01:23:51.150
you're going to measure success.

01:23:51.150 --> 01:23:53.130
Any thoughts on inflation
and particularly,

01:23:53.130 --> 01:23:54.720
how you think you can measure success

01:23:54.720 --> 01:23:56.400
in this massive inflation environment,

01:23:56.400 --> 01:23:57.810
which becomes more challenging.

01:23:57.810 --> 01:23:59.400
- Yes and the inflation environment,

01:23:59.400 --> 01:24:01.287
we're doing a lot of modelling
on that at the moment.

01:24:01.287 --> 01:24:04.144
And it was interesting for inflation.

01:24:04.144 --> 01:24:09.144
We stepped in and worked
intensively with all of the trusts

01:24:09.540 --> 01:24:12.390
when the energy back in the spring,

01:24:12.390 --> 01:24:14.370
when all of a sudden the
energy prices all went up

01:24:14.370 --> 01:24:16.587
because everybody had different tariffs.

01:24:16.587 --> 01:24:17.550
And you know,

01:24:17.550 --> 01:24:19.380
rather than sort of buying
in a particular way.

01:24:19.380 --> 01:24:23.190
So inflation is, and especially
with the NHS Supply Chain

01:24:23.190 --> 01:24:27.120
where we are buying the medical
devices and the consumables,

01:24:27.120 --> 01:24:28.950
we are really, really aware.

01:24:28.950 --> 01:24:32.220
And how does that pricing,
you know, number one,

01:24:32.220 --> 01:24:34.380
where do we accept that
increase in the pricing?

01:24:34.380 --> 01:24:36.360
Because clearly we need the suppliers

01:24:36.360 --> 01:24:40.410
still need to be able to produce
their goods, but equally,

01:24:40.410 --> 01:24:43.620
so then where does that
cost associated go?

01:24:43.620 --> 01:24:45.750
So a lot of work focused on that.

01:24:45.750 --> 01:24:48.390
In terms of how do we measure success,

01:24:48.390 --> 01:24:50.940
we measure success with ultimately,

01:24:50.940 --> 01:24:51.810
there's a couple of ways.

01:24:51.810 --> 01:24:53.070
Of course there's cost.

01:24:53.070 --> 01:24:55.440
I've told you the numbers, you know,

01:24:55.440 --> 01:24:59.580
there is a significant
opportunity to leverage that spend

01:24:59.580 --> 01:25:02.100
and therefore take significant money

01:25:02.100 --> 01:25:03.870
out of our supply chain costs,

01:25:03.870 --> 01:25:05.850
which can go straight
back to the front line.

01:25:05.850 --> 01:25:08.820
So for me, that's the
ultimate level of success,

01:25:08.820 --> 01:25:11.040
but it's not just about the cost.

01:25:11.040 --> 01:25:13.830
It's about the innovation,
it's about the service.

01:25:13.830 --> 01:25:16.473
It's about getting the
products in quicker.

01:25:16.473 --> 01:25:18.150
There's a whole raft of things.

01:25:18.150 --> 01:25:20.160
So we are going to make sure that,

01:25:20.160 --> 01:25:23.430
and I will be bringing back to this board

01:25:23.430 --> 01:25:24.720
hopefully at a regular interval,

01:25:24.720 --> 01:25:25.963
just how we are doing with that.

01:25:25.963 --> 01:25:28.560
- [Richard] Have you
answered Simon's question?

01:25:28.560 --> 01:25:29.563
- Yeah, that was a while ago.

01:25:29.563 --> 01:25:32.904
I think Simon, it was a technology--

01:25:32.904 --> 01:25:34.050
- [Simon] A budget, in the sense.

01:25:34.050 --> 01:25:37.598
- Yeah, it's an interesting
one and that all comes down to

01:25:37.598 --> 01:25:40.950
because we know that it's
not always one size fits all.

01:25:40.950 --> 01:25:42.630
And just to build on Julian's comment,

01:25:42.630 --> 01:25:45.450
you know, so for example,
with some of the technology,

01:25:45.450 --> 01:25:47.010
instead it makes absolute sense

01:25:47.010 --> 01:25:51.900
to buy some of our big federated
things, buy it centrally.

01:25:51.900 --> 01:25:54.570
There are also really, really good reasons

01:25:54.570 --> 01:25:56.970
while we'll continue to do
things at a local level.

01:25:56.970 --> 01:25:59.908
So the answer is we've
got to work through that.

01:25:59.908 --> 01:26:01.934
That's absolutely going
to be one of the things

01:26:01.934 --> 01:26:06.030
that's going to result of
putting the CCF in place.

01:26:06.030 --> 01:26:08.423
- Jacqui, thank you very much.

01:26:08.423 --> 01:26:10.920
I'm going to move us on
to the next agenda item.

01:26:10.920 --> 01:26:13.980
But Jacqui, as you volunteered,
we will be inviting you back

01:26:13.980 --> 01:26:18.153
to give us clearly updates on progress.

01:26:19.320 --> 01:26:22.475
Tim, I think it's you, it's the NHS app.

01:26:22.475 --> 01:26:24.911
You've given us a paper, the same thing.

01:26:24.911 --> 01:26:27.210
I'm assuming the papers
have been read obviously,

01:26:27.210 --> 01:26:29.673
and just draw out the
key highlights for us.

01:26:30.750 --> 01:26:31.911
- Great, thank you very much.

01:26:31.911 --> 01:26:36.911
And so pleased that to
have this discussion here

01:26:37.470 --> 01:26:40.401
at the board, because
this is really critical.

01:26:40.401 --> 01:26:45.401
In your introduction and what
Amanda said about the new NHS,

01:26:46.623 --> 01:26:48.547
this is part of the new NHS.

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:51.840
To connect to what Julian just said,

01:26:51.840 --> 01:26:54.330
this is building on a lot of great work

01:26:54.330 --> 01:26:55.800
that has been done already.

01:26:55.800 --> 01:26:58.770
We've been moving in this
direction for a while,

01:26:58.770 --> 01:27:02.790
but the pandemic showed us
that we can move more quickly

01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:05.760
and more deliberately with focus.

01:27:05.760 --> 01:27:09.287
And I'm so pleased to introduce
Emma McLachlan who is here

01:27:09.287 --> 01:27:12.103
who's providing us with a lot of focus.

01:27:12.103 --> 01:27:14.492
I also just want to acknowledge
Simon Bolton is here,

01:27:14.492 --> 01:27:19.492
the CEO of NHSD whose team has
been doing the technical work

01:27:21.630 --> 01:27:23.520
in collaboration with NHSE.

01:27:23.520 --> 01:27:25.860
I'm so pleased we're all
going to get together.

01:27:25.860 --> 01:27:30.550
Thank you, Laura, for
initiating this process.

01:27:32.070 --> 01:27:34.620
This is about giving the
people of this country

01:27:34.620 --> 01:27:38.040
easier access to trusted
health information,

01:27:38.040 --> 01:27:42.300
especially their own health information.

01:27:42.300 --> 01:27:44.760
I will just say that I
personally have had access

01:27:44.760 --> 01:27:49.760
to my information for
over a decade on my phone.

01:27:50.100 --> 01:27:53.250
100% of what is known about me medically

01:27:53.250 --> 01:27:58.250
is available to me on my
phone or through any computer,

01:27:58.620 --> 01:28:01.440
but I want to make clear this is about

01:28:01.440 --> 01:28:05.910
an additional opportunity
to provide access.

01:28:05.910 --> 01:28:10.890
It is not in replacement
of other existing channels.

01:28:10.890 --> 01:28:13.590
And it's very important to stress that

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:16.810
because we want to make
sure that we're not creating

01:28:17.970 --> 01:28:22.320
any health inequalities in
providing differential access.

01:28:22.320 --> 01:28:25.680
So this is an additional,
and then I just want to say

01:28:25.680 --> 01:28:29.640
that the specific issue of the NHS app,

01:28:29.640 --> 01:28:31.920
which Emma will speak
to you in just a second,

01:28:31.920 --> 01:28:36.920
is we are in a really terrific position.

01:28:37.252 --> 01:28:40.410
I'm sorry to say it this way,
but because of the pandemic,

01:28:40.410 --> 01:28:42.960
because 28 million people

01:28:42.960 --> 01:28:47.160
now have the NHS app on their phone,

01:28:47.160 --> 01:28:48.990
that is an extraordinary percentage

01:28:48.990 --> 01:28:51.120
of the population of this country.

01:28:51.120 --> 01:28:56.120
And also importantly, nearly
two million people have access,

01:28:56.400 --> 01:28:58.020
and this has been slowly building,

01:28:58.020 --> 01:29:01.860
to their own GP health
records through a programme

01:29:01.860 --> 01:29:03.870
that started several years ago

01:29:03.870 --> 01:29:06.720
that has been building slowly.

01:29:06.720 --> 01:29:09.420
And we're hoping to
accelerate that process

01:29:09.420 --> 01:29:11.100
with many more features.

01:29:11.100 --> 01:29:13.110
So, Emma.

01:29:13.110 --> 01:29:14.610
- Thank you, Tim.

01:29:14.610 --> 01:29:16.740
Thank you, it's great
to be here at the board

01:29:16.740 --> 01:29:18.480
having a conversation about this.

01:29:18.480 --> 01:29:20.970
So yeah, just to follow on
from Tim's introduction there,

01:29:20.970 --> 01:29:21.803
you know,

01:29:21.803 --> 01:29:24.480
we have obviously seen
unprecedented scaling of the NHS app

01:29:24.480 --> 01:29:25.590
as a core national challenge.

01:29:25.590 --> 01:29:28.714
- Emma, just for me, you may
have to speak up a little bit.

01:29:28.714 --> 01:29:29.700
Sorry about that.

01:29:29.700 --> 01:29:30.760
- No problem.

01:29:30.760 --> 01:29:33.120
I said to echo Tim's introduction here,

01:29:33.120 --> 01:29:35.640
we have seen unprecedented
scaling of the NHS app

01:29:35.640 --> 01:29:37.950
over the course of the
last 12 to 18 months.

01:29:37.950 --> 01:29:39.874
And we now see close to a million people

01:29:39.874 --> 01:29:42.630
logging into that
service every single day.

01:29:42.630 --> 01:29:43.470
You know, that's on a par

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:45.150
with a lot of big high street brands,

01:29:45.150 --> 01:29:47.580
and obviously it's an
enormous opportunity for us

01:29:47.580 --> 01:29:48.930
and demonstrates clear appetite

01:29:48.930 --> 01:29:50.760
for our national channels going forward.

01:29:50.760 --> 01:29:52.310
So it's great to be on the map.

01:29:53.310 --> 01:29:55.200
And also I think when we
last spoke at the board

01:29:55.200 --> 01:29:57.570
about the NHS app and
the national channels,

01:29:57.570 --> 01:29:58.890
there was concern that perhaps

01:29:58.890 --> 01:30:00.840
we may be all about COVID past,

01:30:00.840 --> 01:30:02.910
but I'm happy to come
back and tell this board

01:30:02.910 --> 01:30:05.430
that we are moving on from that.

01:30:05.430 --> 01:30:08.190
We are seeing steady,
incremental growth week on week

01:30:08.190 --> 01:30:10.470
of new people signing up for the NHS app.

01:30:10.470 --> 01:30:12.480
And the majority of what
people are doing now

01:30:12.480 --> 01:30:14.600
is interactions with core services.

01:30:14.600 --> 01:30:15.990
So that's repeat prescriptions,

01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:17.970
it's record access as Tim has mentioned,

01:30:17.970 --> 01:30:20.310
it's appointment booking and a lot more.

01:30:20.310 --> 01:30:21.390
And we're also seeing

01:30:21.390 --> 01:30:23.940
out of that 28 million registered users,

01:30:23.940 --> 01:30:26.130
a third of people coming
back month on month.

01:30:26.130 --> 01:30:27.810
And that seems to be a steady state now

01:30:27.810 --> 01:30:28.770
for the last quarter.

01:30:28.770 --> 01:30:30.900
It's something we need to
obviously keep an eye on,

01:30:30.900 --> 01:30:32.490
but I think that's a
really encouraging position

01:30:32.490 --> 01:30:33.993
to be in at this point.

01:30:36.090 --> 01:30:38.160
So the important bit is what
are we going to do next?

01:30:38.160 --> 01:30:41.970
And you'll have seen that
we published the app roadmap

01:30:41.970 --> 01:30:44.100
with the digital health
and care plan last week,

01:30:44.100 --> 01:30:46.880
which gave a comprehensive
list of the appetite we have

01:30:46.880 --> 01:30:50.610
on the backlog for services
to increase the availability

01:30:50.610 --> 01:30:52.530
of what people can do to self serve

01:30:52.530 --> 01:30:54.565
and self-care through this channel.

01:30:54.565 --> 01:30:57.930
We have picked specifically
items this year

01:30:57.930 --> 01:31:00.540
that will support large
volumes of people and things

01:31:00.540 --> 01:31:02.280
we can genuinely deliver to.

01:31:02.280 --> 01:31:04.295
And that talks to the COVID vaccines,

01:31:04.295 --> 01:31:06.630
which you obviously mentioned
earlier in the meeting

01:31:06.630 --> 01:31:08.285
being of such importance.

01:31:08.285 --> 01:31:11.170
It continues the increase

01:31:11.170 --> 01:31:14.940
in people's availability
to access GP data.

01:31:14.940 --> 01:31:15.930
So from later this year,

01:31:15.930 --> 01:31:18.600
all perspective and new
information going into GP record

01:31:18.600 --> 01:31:20.820
will be available through the NHS app.

01:31:20.820 --> 01:31:22.950
And we're also looking to join up

01:31:22.950 --> 01:31:24.570
hospital appointment information

01:31:24.570 --> 01:31:27.750
and make a start into the
in road with that journey

01:31:27.750 --> 01:31:30.100
over the course of this
financial year as well.

01:31:31.080 --> 01:31:33.240
But it's not just about
increasing services

01:31:33.240 --> 01:31:34.440
that we need to do.

01:31:34.440 --> 01:31:36.270
And also to echo the start of your point,

01:31:36.270 --> 01:31:39.060
this is about improving
the experience of the NHS

01:31:39.060 --> 01:31:40.290
that we offer to people,

01:31:40.290 --> 01:31:43.620
about joining things together
around them as an individual.

01:31:43.620 --> 01:31:44.550
And the app at the moment

01:31:44.550 --> 01:31:46.140
is actually linked to a provider set.

01:31:46.140 --> 01:31:48.747
We actually identify users based on GP.

01:31:48.747 --> 01:31:51.120
And we need to start to
think about our end users,

01:31:51.120 --> 01:31:52.377
the individuals that they are,

01:31:52.377 --> 01:31:54.450
and actually start to hang the services

01:31:54.450 --> 01:31:56.556
and the access of
information that they need

01:31:56.556 --> 01:31:59.550
that's relevant to them
off the back of that,

01:31:59.550 --> 01:32:02.430
be that their preferences on
how they want to be spoken to,

01:32:02.430 --> 01:32:04.950
be that who they care for
and who they look after,

01:32:04.950 --> 01:32:06.210
be it their location,

01:32:06.210 --> 01:32:08.910
potentially their medical
condition and other things.

01:32:08.910 --> 01:32:10.860
And that's the journey that
we need to take ourselves on

01:32:10.860 --> 01:32:12.270
with the NHS app now,

01:32:12.270 --> 01:32:15.090
and also across our wider
digital channels too.

01:32:15.090 --> 01:32:17.880
It's not all only about the mobile,

01:32:17.880 --> 01:32:18.780
but I'm going to pause there

01:32:18.780 --> 01:32:20.550
'cos I know there'll probably
be comments and questions,

01:32:20.550 --> 01:32:21.383
so thank you.

01:32:21.383 --> 01:32:22.216
- I'm sure there will be.

01:32:22.216 --> 01:32:25.319
So actually, well, I'll point
out you've got the Chair

01:32:25.319 --> 01:32:28.980
and the Chief Exec of Digital
sitting opposite of you,

01:32:28.980 --> 01:32:31.378
both hands went up immediately.

01:32:31.378 --> 01:32:35.100
I don't mind which order you
go, Chair or CO first, but.

01:32:35.967 --> 01:32:37.593
- All right, ladies first.

01:32:39.000 --> 01:32:40.830
Well, first of all, I just want to say,

01:32:40.830 --> 01:32:45.090
I think it is remarkable
where we have come from,

01:32:45.090 --> 01:32:49.290
and too, not just in the
app, but actually nhs.co.uk

01:32:49.290 --> 01:32:53.190
in terms of a trusted source
of health information.

01:32:53.190 --> 01:32:56.337
In today's kind of world, I
think that's really important.

01:32:56.337 --> 01:32:58.290
And the innovation
that's been shown there,

01:32:58.290 --> 01:33:00.985
so working with search
engines to get little snippets

01:33:00.985 --> 01:33:03.840
up of the NHS so it is
the trusted authority.

01:33:03.840 --> 01:33:05.040
I think that's terrific.

01:33:05.910 --> 01:33:09.300
The second thing I'd say
is you talk in the paper

01:33:09.300 --> 01:33:13.200
about wanting to understand
behaviour and usage

01:33:13.200 --> 01:33:15.180
at a sort of more detailed level.

01:33:15.180 --> 01:33:17.400
And I think it's lovely to have Bola here

01:33:17.400 --> 01:33:19.650
because I think this links to this point

01:33:19.650 --> 01:33:22.800
about how do we continue to press on,

01:33:22.800 --> 01:33:25.320
making sure that our digital channels

01:33:25.320 --> 01:33:27.207
are accessible as possible,

01:33:27.207 --> 01:33:29.940
as well as ensuring
they're all alternatives.

01:33:29.940 --> 01:33:32.370
And I think that that
work around accessibility

01:33:32.370 --> 01:33:33.690
and how we do use a design

01:33:33.690 --> 01:33:35.640
and really understanding it's crucial,

01:33:35.640 --> 01:33:37.203
so I commend you on that.

01:33:38.130 --> 01:33:41.940
But my main point is those
of us who've been responsible

01:33:41.940 --> 01:33:44.820
for kind of those big high street apps,

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:46.320
or indeed as citizens.

01:33:46.320 --> 01:33:49.110
We all know that why you use
an app is when it's useful,

01:33:49.110 --> 01:33:50.790
not when it's worthy.

01:33:50.790 --> 01:33:55.380
So I think, and your paper
highlights that where citizens

01:33:55.380 --> 01:33:58.890
can actually do things
like book appointments,

01:33:58.890 --> 01:34:03.840
join up local services, that's
what unlocks the real value.

01:34:03.840 --> 01:34:07.740
And so my question, my two
questions for you really,

01:34:07.740 --> 01:34:12.740
one is, we know that
and your paper is full

01:34:13.020 --> 01:34:17.010
of where providers have signed
up, where GPS have signed up.

01:34:17.010 --> 01:34:20.130
How do we accelerate
and remove that proviso

01:34:20.130 --> 01:34:23.250
from where they've signed
up to they have signed up

01:34:23.250 --> 01:34:26.010
so we can get that useful journey?

01:34:26.010 --> 01:34:30.840
And the second is where
does 111 online fit in here,

01:34:30.840 --> 01:34:33.330
because this paper is silent on it

01:34:33.330 --> 01:34:36.347
and I think it's hugely
important that we are clear

01:34:36.347 --> 01:34:40.080
and we develop that in
as focused a manner.

01:34:40.080 --> 01:34:43.260
- So can you hold and note the questions.

01:34:43.260 --> 01:34:45.147
I'll go and pick up a
few for you from Simon,

01:34:45.147 --> 01:34:46.710
and then I think David's got question.

01:34:46.710 --> 01:34:47.543
- I'll make it easy for you

01:34:47.543 --> 01:34:49.256
'cos this is an observation
for the board actually

01:34:49.256 --> 01:34:50.510
rather than a question,

01:34:50.510 --> 01:34:53.283
which I think it is really
important for us to all recognise

01:34:53.283 --> 01:34:57.660
that this signals us moving
away from kind of actually

01:34:57.660 --> 01:34:59.490
the way that technology
used to be delivered

01:34:59.490 --> 01:35:00.323
from the centre.

01:35:00.323 --> 01:35:02.520
We delivered it to providers and trusts.

01:35:02.520 --> 01:35:05.580
We're now talking about delivering
technology to consumers,

01:35:05.580 --> 01:35:08.003
so moving to that business
to consumer model.

01:35:08.003 --> 01:35:10.920
And I don't know about
any of you, but you know,

01:35:10.920 --> 01:35:12.960
10 years ago, well maybe 15 years ago,

01:35:12.960 --> 01:35:16.470
I definitely wasn't using my
phone to interact with my bank.

01:35:16.470 --> 01:35:18.420
Now I completely depend upon it.

01:35:18.420 --> 01:35:21.660
The more we develop the
capability within the app,

01:35:21.660 --> 01:35:23.370
the more our customers,

01:35:23.370 --> 01:35:26.008
our patients are going to rely on this.

01:35:26.008 --> 01:35:28.703
So our ability to deliver

01:35:28.703 --> 01:35:31.050
what I call consumer grade service,

01:35:31.050 --> 01:35:33.720
which actually arguably is a
much higher level of service

01:35:33.720 --> 01:35:36.398
than enterprise grade service these days,

01:35:36.398 --> 01:35:38.730
becomes really, really significant.

01:35:38.730 --> 01:35:42.870
So this is always on, always
available, highly secure.

01:35:42.870 --> 01:35:46.530
That requires us to develop and maintain

01:35:46.530 --> 01:35:48.780
a level of capability at the centre,

01:35:48.780 --> 01:35:50.460
which is really significant.

01:35:50.460 --> 01:35:52.590
- So thank you for that comment,

01:35:52.590 --> 01:35:54.656
and I agree ho wholeheartedly.

01:35:54.656 --> 01:35:56.790
David, your question.

01:35:56.790 --> 01:35:58.170
- I really enjoyed reading this.

01:35:58.170 --> 01:36:01.763
I think it's terrific, so congratulations.

01:36:01.763 --> 01:36:04.230
I think I might be asking
the same question as Laura.

01:36:04.230 --> 01:36:07.180
So forgive me if I am,
but peppered throughout,

01:36:07.180 --> 01:36:09.030
a really good time table

01:36:09.030 --> 01:36:10.710
of what's going to get delivered by when,

01:36:10.710 --> 01:36:13.603
which I also really appreciated,

01:36:13.603 --> 01:36:18.603
things like if my local
hospital is participating.

01:36:19.110 --> 01:36:21.262
And it seems to me, I'd
just like to understand.

01:36:21.262 --> 01:36:26.262
I've got mine open and it's very difficult

01:36:27.480 --> 01:36:28.740
to find some of this stuff.

01:36:28.740 --> 01:36:30.870
I've had treatment over
the past 12 months,

01:36:30.870 --> 01:36:32.793
which is just not on the app.

01:36:34.590 --> 01:36:37.557
And I suspect it's
because my local systems

01:36:37.557 --> 01:36:38.970
are not participating in this,

01:36:38.970 --> 01:36:41.880
and therefore the utility
to deliver the vision

01:36:41.880 --> 01:36:45.840
that Simon has just outlined
is going to dependent on that.

01:36:45.840 --> 01:36:47.992
The last conversation,
which was also terrific,

01:36:47.992 --> 01:36:50.488
we talked about the
importance of standardisation

01:36:50.488 --> 01:36:52.830
and getting the benefits
from standardisation.

01:36:52.830 --> 01:36:56.610
I just wondered whether this
is a similar kind of space

01:36:56.610 --> 01:37:00.270
about standardisation and
whether we can go at the pace

01:37:00.270 --> 01:37:02.220
of when everybody gets around to it,

01:37:02.220 --> 01:37:04.950
as opposed to when we need to do it.

01:37:04.950 --> 01:37:09.300
So is this organic change

01:37:09.300 --> 01:37:11.280
or is this transformational change?

01:37:11.280 --> 01:37:16.280
And I just really want to
better understand whether,

01:37:18.150 --> 01:37:20.340
if my local hospital in this case

01:37:20.340 --> 01:37:24.600
is the one that I'm looking
at, has participated or not

01:37:24.600 --> 01:37:28.530
affects whether I can be part
of this revolution or not.

01:37:28.530 --> 01:37:30.720
- So I'm going to take
two more questions for you

01:37:30.720 --> 01:37:32.550
and then ask you to deal with them.

01:37:32.550 --> 01:37:35.160
So Jeremy has a question, and
Bola has a question I think.

01:37:35.160 --> 01:37:36.210
- Building on that point,

01:37:36.210 --> 01:37:39.960
I think this is a major
opportunity to drive effectiveness,

01:37:39.960 --> 01:37:42.300
not least getting the
patient and the customer

01:37:42.300 --> 01:37:44.640
into the right place in the system,

01:37:44.640 --> 01:37:46.170
which could be phenomenal.

01:37:46.170 --> 01:37:47.214
The other request I've got,

01:37:47.214 --> 01:37:50.580
I'm not sure who I would
aim this request out,

01:37:50.580 --> 01:37:52.200
but I think this could
equally be effective

01:37:52.200 --> 01:37:56.190
for colleagues and nurses,
doctors, back office staff,

01:37:56.190 --> 01:37:58.319
and there could be massive
efficiencies driven.

01:37:58.319 --> 01:38:01.530
Certainly in my experience
in the commercial world,

01:38:01.530 --> 01:38:03.630
we drove some real
effectiveness and efficiency

01:38:03.630 --> 01:38:06.690
by having colleagues linked
in as well as patients.

01:38:06.690 --> 01:38:10.500
So probably request into
the system somewhere

01:38:10.500 --> 01:38:11.820
as part of the efficiency review

01:38:11.820 --> 01:38:13.560
looking at where that might deliver

01:38:13.560 --> 01:38:16.440
and how we could take waste and redundancy

01:38:16.440 --> 01:38:21.440
out of our medical staff, the
nursing staff and doctors.

01:38:21.531 --> 01:38:23.790
I'd like to add to the list.

01:38:23.790 --> 01:38:27.420
- And then lastly, Bola,
you have a question.

01:38:27.420 --> 01:38:28.253
- Thank you, Richard.

01:38:28.253 --> 01:38:30.810
And I'm similarly welcoming the paper,

01:38:30.810 --> 01:38:32.610
particularly the focus on accessibility

01:38:32.610 --> 01:38:34.650
as Laura was just saying.

01:38:34.650 --> 01:38:36.840
I think it's an offer of support

01:38:36.840 --> 01:38:39.270
as well as a request at the same time.

01:38:39.270 --> 01:38:41.730
So the digital health and care plan

01:38:41.730 --> 01:38:43.140
that was published two weeks ago

01:38:43.140 --> 01:38:45.690
that Julian referred to earlier on,

01:38:45.690 --> 01:38:49.380
one of the key deliverables in
that is that we will develop

01:38:49.380 --> 01:38:53.910
a digital inclusion framework by 2023 May.

01:38:53.910 --> 01:38:56.730
So it's just as we
develop the app further,

01:38:56.730 --> 01:38:58.857
how do we incorporate
the principles of that?

01:38:58.857 --> 01:39:01.890
And we're very keen to support
the development of that.

01:39:01.890 --> 01:39:04.890
And visa versa, your expertise
in terms of developing

01:39:04.890 --> 01:39:07.140
that framework so that it's user friendly

01:39:07.140 --> 01:39:09.390
and it supports the
accelerated development

01:39:09.390 --> 01:39:10.710
of the app as well.

01:39:10.710 --> 01:39:12.600
- So do you want to have
a go those questions?

01:39:12.600 --> 01:39:14.861
And then I know Steve's got a comment,

01:39:14.861 --> 01:39:16.950
but you have a go at
those questions first.

01:39:16.950 --> 01:39:19.466
Otherwise you're going to have a

01:39:19.466 --> 01:39:20.910
hell of a list to get through.

01:39:20.910 --> 01:39:24.648
- Yeah, well fortunately
there were some themes.

01:39:24.648 --> 01:39:25.893
- [Richard] Well done.

01:39:27.390 --> 01:39:31.830
- And one of them, I want to
make sure that I emphasise

01:39:31.830 --> 01:39:35.340
one really important point
related to multiple questions

01:39:35.340 --> 01:39:39.780
on I think where providers signed up.

01:39:39.780 --> 01:39:43.440
That might not have been
actually optimal language

01:39:43.440 --> 01:39:46.773
because it's not necessarily
about where they signed up.

01:39:47.730 --> 01:39:50.370
As many of the members of this board know,

01:39:50.370 --> 01:39:55.370
there are 32 trusts in this
country that don't have an EPR.

01:39:55.800 --> 01:39:57.540
If you don't have an EPR,

01:39:57.540 --> 01:40:00.633
you can't front the data into an app.

01:40:02.340 --> 01:40:07.340
Also very significantly,
another 50% of trusts

01:40:07.710 --> 01:40:10.661
in this country have
EPRs that do not support

01:40:10.661 --> 01:40:14.640
the kind of APIs necessary to do this.

01:40:14.640 --> 01:40:19.640
That's a lot of trusts that
are working in tech that is,

01:40:22.080 --> 01:40:24.210
let's just say outdated.

01:40:24.210 --> 01:40:28.350
And so modern tech has something called...

01:40:28.350 --> 01:40:30.570
It comes with something
called Fire Standards.

01:40:30.570 --> 01:40:33.900
Fire Standards are the
international standards

01:40:33.900 --> 01:40:38.880
for health information transmission.

01:40:38.880 --> 01:40:41.970
All modern vendors and all current vendors

01:40:41.970 --> 01:40:45.810
have the capability of providing
APIs for Fire Standards.

01:40:45.810 --> 01:40:50.520
Those make signing up to this
really easy, like really easy.

01:40:50.520 --> 01:40:54.690
Not necessarily no work, but
a minimal amount of work.

01:40:54.690 --> 01:40:59.690
We have got to get all of
our care delivery on tech

01:41:01.590 --> 01:41:04.890
that is enabled through modern APIs.

01:41:04.890 --> 01:41:06.690
And we are a ways off from that.

01:41:06.690 --> 01:41:09.780
I need to be perfectly transparent.

01:41:09.780 --> 01:41:12.480
And so it's not necessarily
about signing up.

01:41:12.480 --> 01:41:15.450
It's that the tech infrastructure

01:41:15.450 --> 01:41:18.540
needs to be there to support it.

01:41:18.540 --> 01:41:21.570
- Okay, so the part two of this

01:41:21.570 --> 01:41:26.570
was the current policy
around getting access.

01:41:28.680 --> 01:41:31.470
So the two main providers,

01:41:31.470 --> 01:41:35.550
and actually I think all of
the providers for GP Systems,

01:41:35.550 --> 01:41:37.380
which are honestly,

01:41:37.380 --> 01:41:39.540
when people go searching
after health information,

01:41:39.540 --> 01:41:41.850
it is typically about themselves.

01:41:41.850 --> 01:41:43.800
It is typically the
information that's contained

01:41:43.800 --> 01:41:47.437
in their GP record that is most relevant,

01:41:47.437 --> 01:41:50.190
I would guess to a majority of people.

01:41:50.190 --> 01:41:55.190
And the current policy on
that is if a patient requests

01:41:56.850 --> 01:42:01.320
of their GP, the GP has the
ability to turn them on.

01:42:01.320 --> 01:42:03.780
This is a one by one process.

01:42:03.780 --> 01:42:05.953
So I mentioned at the beginning

01:42:05.953 --> 01:42:07.620
that almost two million people

01:42:07.620 --> 01:42:11.610
have access to their
GP record information.

01:42:11.610 --> 01:42:14.820
That's because there was a
transaction that occurred

01:42:14.820 --> 01:42:17.123
that they said, I want this turned on,

01:42:17.123 --> 01:42:20.670
and the GP enabled it and turned it on.

01:42:20.670 --> 01:42:25.080
We are working with our
colleagues at RCGP, BMA,

01:42:25.080 --> 01:42:30.080
and colleagues in primary
care for a process this fall

01:42:31.020 --> 01:42:34.890
that allows, and we are
building the tech to do this,

01:42:34.890 --> 01:42:38.880
that allows an entire
practise to turn this on

01:42:38.880 --> 01:42:41.460
for prospective coded data.

01:42:41.460 --> 01:42:42.900
So not everything that's in the chart,

01:42:42.900 --> 01:42:45.030
but actually probably
the most important bits

01:42:45.030 --> 01:42:46.410
that are in the chart.

01:42:46.410 --> 01:42:48.990
And we hope to have that process completed

01:42:48.990 --> 01:42:50.283
by the end of November.

01:42:52.111 --> 01:42:53.520
So that's the more detail

01:42:53.520 --> 01:42:57.660
on the specific timetable
that is in the paper.

01:42:57.660 --> 01:43:02.660
And so soon I think is the
answer Laura, on that question.

01:43:03.600 --> 01:43:08.187
Yeah, and then apologies, do you want to?

01:43:08.187 --> 01:43:09.190
- No, I was just going to say,

01:43:09.190 --> 01:43:12.150
as someone responsible for the NHS app,

01:43:12.150 --> 01:43:13.800
we often get asked the
questions that actually

01:43:13.800 --> 01:43:15.690
are exactly the mirror to the challenges

01:43:15.690 --> 01:43:17.040
that we have elsewhere.

01:43:17.040 --> 01:43:19.620
And we can only surface what we can see.

01:43:19.620 --> 01:43:22.890
And so in prioritising, for
example, delivery in year,

01:43:22.890 --> 01:43:24.690
looking at what we can do for mass benefit

01:43:24.690 --> 01:43:25.800
for the most number of people,

01:43:25.800 --> 01:43:28.170
we have to look first at
what data is available

01:43:28.170 --> 01:43:29.520
that can be surfaced out.

01:43:29.520 --> 01:43:30.810
And indeed, that's how we've gone

01:43:30.810 --> 01:43:32.670
about prioritising our roadmap,

01:43:32.670 --> 01:43:35.820
but we must also make inroads
on some of those harder areas

01:43:35.820 --> 01:43:38.040
in order to start that journey of join up.

01:43:38.040 --> 01:43:40.290
Hence why, you know, to your point,

01:43:40.290 --> 01:43:41.940
perhaps we could finesse the language,

01:43:41.940 --> 01:43:45.455
but that's where it's
starting from, kind of stems.

01:43:45.455 --> 01:43:46.805
Thank you for the question.

01:43:48.784 --> 01:43:50.600
- [Richard] Do you want to
ask your questions Steve?

01:43:50.600 --> 01:43:52.260
- It's related to Jeremy's question,

01:43:52.260 --> 01:43:55.500
which I think was around how
you could bring some benefit

01:43:55.500 --> 01:43:58.620
to staff and some staff facing
applications into the app.

01:43:58.620 --> 01:44:00.120
And it just struck me.

01:44:00.120 --> 01:44:01.680
And this goes back to
the heart of the question

01:44:01.680 --> 01:44:03.210
we were having earlier
about what we do centrally

01:44:03.210 --> 01:44:04.680
and what we don't do.

01:44:04.680 --> 01:44:07.470
Of course, there are lots
of digital apps there in use

01:44:07.470 --> 01:44:10.140
in the NHS that are
developed commercially,

01:44:10.140 --> 01:44:12.288
often by small entrepreneurs,

01:44:12.288 --> 01:44:14.730
from everything from outpatient management

01:44:14.730 --> 01:44:16.950
to rota management to task management.

01:44:16.950 --> 01:44:18.750
In fact, many of them we support ourselves

01:44:18.750 --> 01:44:19.680
through our own innovations,

01:44:19.680 --> 01:44:22.030
accelerator and clinical
entrepreneurs programme.

01:44:22.890 --> 01:44:25.230
So there's always a balance
between what we decide

01:44:25.230 --> 01:44:27.150
what to do and what we decide
that we're not going to do

01:44:27.150 --> 01:44:29.160
because it's better done by somebody else.

01:44:29.160 --> 01:44:31.290
And I just wondered where you thought

01:44:31.290 --> 01:44:33.900
that balance needs to be going forward.

01:44:33.900 --> 01:44:35.825
- That's a great question.

01:44:35.825 --> 01:44:37.110
Should I take that or is there--

01:44:37.110 --> 01:44:37.943
- Will you hold it a minute?

01:44:37.943 --> 01:44:39.390
Munir has got the last question, I think.

01:44:39.390 --> 01:44:40.223
- Okay.

01:44:40.223 --> 01:44:41.263
- Sorry, yeah.

01:44:41.263 --> 01:44:42.713
A great paper, and you know,

01:44:42.713 --> 01:44:45.960
fantastic to see the development
of the app and so on.

01:44:45.960 --> 01:44:48.330
So I was particularly keen
to look to the future.

01:44:48.330 --> 01:44:51.060
And in 2025, you say you're
going to have a genomic profile.

01:44:51.060 --> 01:44:53.783
Can you give more detail on
exactly what you mean by that?

01:44:55.560 --> 01:44:57.560
- I should have predicted that question.

01:44:59.880 --> 01:45:01.650
Let me take on what we build

01:45:01.650 --> 01:45:06.650
versus what we sort of let
the ecosystem build for us.

01:45:07.950 --> 01:45:10.980
That is really a great question.

01:45:10.980 --> 01:45:13.945
There are some complexities around that,

01:45:13.945 --> 01:45:18.330
'cos I know Emma sort of prepared
herself for that question,

01:45:18.330 --> 01:45:23.330
but let me just say that
we have not always taken

01:45:23.610 --> 01:45:26.640
the same strategy with
respect to that question.

01:45:26.640 --> 01:45:31.640
And my own personal view
is it's an and, not an or,

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:37.080
that there is room for us
to invite other players

01:45:37.080 --> 01:45:37.913
in this market.

01:45:37.913 --> 01:45:39.690
I will just say Milton Keynes

01:45:39.690 --> 01:45:41.880
has a great relationship with Apple.

01:45:41.880 --> 01:45:43.744
And Apple actually fronts the app

01:45:43.744 --> 01:45:47.310
for all the patients
seen in Milton Keynes.

01:45:47.310 --> 01:45:51.840
That kind of relationship,
we should have more of those

01:45:51.840 --> 01:45:55.115
because that will speed access.

01:45:55.115 --> 01:45:58.830
I will just say on the other
hand though, it does take...

01:45:58.830 --> 01:46:02.790
If you really want one place
to go for all of your data,

01:46:02.790 --> 01:46:07.290
that will require a national
centralised programme.

01:46:07.290 --> 01:46:10.500
I think both, there's
absolutely room for both

01:46:10.500 --> 01:46:12.660
and we should move into that territory.

01:46:12.660 --> 01:46:13.493
Emma, do you want to?

01:46:13.493 --> 01:46:14.790
- I was just going to say,

01:46:14.790 --> 01:46:17.460
I think when the NHS app was conceived

01:46:17.460 --> 01:46:18.480
that there were lots of discussions

01:46:18.480 --> 01:46:21.120
about potential joint ventures
and all sorts of things.

01:46:21.120 --> 01:46:22.680
And that predates me,

01:46:22.680 --> 01:46:24.600
but I'm sure there were some
very sensible rationales

01:46:24.600 --> 01:46:26.880
around that at the time.

01:46:26.880 --> 01:46:29.160
It's a good question to
raise, I think around that,

01:46:29.160 --> 01:46:31.980
and something we should
be further considering.

01:46:31.980 --> 01:46:32.880
But in addition to that,

01:46:32.880 --> 01:46:34.980
we need to think about what
the role of the NHS app

01:46:34.980 --> 01:46:36.960
in our national channels is here to do,

01:46:36.960 --> 01:46:39.180
which I thought talk specifically to your,

01:46:39.180 --> 01:46:42.000
what's the role of the market,
perhaps in managing pathways,

01:46:42.000 --> 01:46:44.340
looking at individual
component parts of what we need

01:46:44.340 --> 01:46:46.440
to support our health and care system,

01:46:46.440 --> 01:46:48.690
both from a staff and a
patient point of view.

01:46:48.690 --> 01:46:51.360
And we won't be able to do
everything, nor is it appropriate

01:46:51.360 --> 01:46:52.890
that we should do everything nationally.

01:46:52.890 --> 01:46:55.200
You know, we are
deliberately at this moment,

01:46:55.200 --> 01:46:59.010
a thin layer that we want
the market to innovate

01:46:59.010 --> 01:47:02.010
in and around so that we
can provide that platform

01:47:02.010 --> 01:47:04.620
to surface those innovations through.

01:47:04.620 --> 01:47:06.654
But I think we need to think
further now as we've scaled

01:47:06.654 --> 01:47:09.840
and now our ambitions and
the opportunity is greater.

01:47:09.840 --> 01:47:10.920
How does that work?

01:47:10.920 --> 01:47:12.870
Because there must be
some lines in the sand

01:47:12.870 --> 01:47:14.460
where we need to give
clarity to the market

01:47:14.460 --> 01:47:15.990
and what we want them to invest in

01:47:15.990 --> 01:47:18.043
versus what we will invest in centrally.

01:47:18.043 --> 01:47:19.080
- Thank you.

01:47:19.080 --> 01:47:19.913
Very quickly,

01:47:19.913 --> 01:47:23.280
would you answer Munir's question as well?

01:47:23.280 --> 01:47:28.280
- So genomics, so there's a
really big opportunity here.

01:47:29.040 --> 01:47:30.240
This is what I would call

01:47:30.240 --> 01:47:32.280
more at the cutting edge of innovation.

01:47:32.280 --> 01:47:36.720
So even how you front,
to use Emma's language,

01:47:36.720 --> 01:47:40.050
how you surface this
information to patients

01:47:40.050 --> 01:47:41.191
is self evolving.

01:47:41.191 --> 01:47:44.130
It's evolved actually quite
a bit in the last two years.

01:47:44.130 --> 01:47:47.820
And so we want to stay
very close to that market,

01:47:47.820 --> 01:47:51.900
but when you're at that
leading edge of innovation,

01:47:51.900 --> 01:47:54.480
that's really where you
want to have partners

01:47:54.480 --> 01:47:59.480
in the process, because there
are some really innovative

01:48:01.350 --> 01:48:04.680
tech companies that are
clearly leading in this space

01:48:04.680 --> 01:48:08.610
and it's a complicated space to be in.

01:48:08.610 --> 01:48:10.055
So it's probably not something

01:48:10.055 --> 01:48:12.270
until it becomes more of a commodity,

01:48:12.270 --> 01:48:15.376
which I don't think will
happen for some time.

01:48:15.376 --> 01:48:18.153
It's an area we will want to partner with.

01:48:20.640 --> 01:48:21.987
- Thank you for a very good paper

01:48:21.987 --> 01:48:24.423
and for a very good discussion.

01:48:25.290 --> 01:48:26.123
I'm going to move this on,

01:48:26.123 --> 01:48:30.409
but I will leave just
two thoughts I think,

01:48:30.409 --> 01:48:32.905
which I'm not looking for a response to.

01:48:32.905 --> 01:48:35.273
I mean, the thing for
me is how do we drive

01:48:35.273 --> 01:48:38.040
even greater utilisation of the NHS app?

01:48:38.040 --> 01:48:41.310
How do I get the whole
population going there

01:48:41.310 --> 01:48:44.987
as a really regular app that
they go to for information?

01:48:44.987 --> 01:48:47.280
And is that because you
have to use partnerships,

01:48:47.280 --> 01:48:51.060
or what is it that excites use?

01:48:51.060 --> 01:48:54.450
And the second thing for me
picks up by the Apple point.

01:48:54.450 --> 01:48:55.753
And we've talked about it before Tim.

01:48:55.753 --> 01:48:59.940
And it's a wild thought
probably, but you know,

01:48:59.940 --> 01:49:04.170
if I'm one of the major technology players

01:49:04.170 --> 01:49:07.980
and I think I can get hold of a 28 million

01:49:07.980 --> 01:49:12.980
naturally refreshing group of individuals,

01:49:13.230 --> 01:49:14.910
the adult population,

01:49:14.910 --> 01:49:17.643
largely the adult
population of this country.

01:49:20.675 --> 01:49:22.410
And actually from our perspective,

01:49:22.410 --> 01:49:24.960
you have a single client view, frankly.

01:49:24.960 --> 01:49:26.400
You have a single customer identifier

01:49:26.400 --> 01:49:27.900
that most financial services companies

01:49:27.900 --> 01:49:29.820
would get their hind
teeth to get a hold of,

01:49:29.820 --> 01:49:31.593
which actually is your NHS number.

01:49:32.520 --> 01:49:35.430
This is a remarkably
powerful potential tool

01:49:35.430 --> 01:49:39.270
I think where we could
think about partnerships.

01:49:39.270 --> 01:49:43.470
And those partnerships could
bring the habit of use,

01:49:43.470 --> 01:49:45.300
as opposed to, if you think about it

01:49:45.300 --> 01:49:47.250
this way around as we do,
like it's the NHS app.

01:49:47.250 --> 01:49:52.250
So can I do my GP appointment
or look at my historic record.

01:49:53.550 --> 01:49:55.050
how do I drive real use?

01:49:55.050 --> 01:49:57.360
And is it partnerships, what is it?

01:49:57.360 --> 01:49:59.730
But this is an extremely valuable,

01:49:59.730 --> 01:50:02.975
amazingly valuable entry point

01:50:02.975 --> 01:50:06.180
to an adult population
that continues to refresh

01:50:06.180 --> 01:50:08.790
in a world which is continuing to evolve

01:50:08.790 --> 01:50:10.680
and use more and more of these apps.

01:50:10.680 --> 01:50:13.110
And then the last one
will be, imagine the world

01:50:13.110 --> 01:50:15.722
if we had the centre led
or centralised standards

01:50:15.722 --> 01:50:18.120
and controlled where you opened it up

01:50:18.120 --> 01:50:20.490
to all sorts of third party developers

01:50:20.490 --> 01:50:21.630
who are sitting in their rooms,

01:50:21.630 --> 01:50:23.607
doing development 'cos
they're interested in that.

01:50:23.607 --> 01:50:27.570
And it could open up a world
of real information flow,

01:50:27.570 --> 01:50:30.660
available, local, relevant information.

01:50:30.660 --> 01:50:32.840
You know, how long is my
waiting time if I go to the ED

01:50:32.840 --> 01:50:34.200
of a particular hospital at the moment?

01:50:34.200 --> 01:50:36.150
Or whatever it happens to be.

01:50:36.150 --> 01:50:39.630
We should be able to develop
those capabilities I think.

01:50:39.630 --> 01:50:41.970
Anyway, it's a really good
paper and thank you very much,

01:50:41.970 --> 01:50:44.490
and we're going to get to the
last item and I do apologise.

01:50:44.490 --> 01:50:48.150
I try to keep us the time,
but we still have got time.

01:50:48.150 --> 01:50:51.030
And we've got Habib Naqvi
with us, thank you very much.

01:50:51.030 --> 01:50:55.440
He's the director of the Race
and Health Observatory, NHS.

01:50:55.440 --> 01:50:57.598
And also Bola Owolabi,

01:50:57.598 --> 01:51:01.500
who's the Director of
Healthcare Inequalities.

01:51:01.500 --> 01:51:02.520
And you've given us a paper,

01:51:02.520 --> 01:51:04.590
which is the annual report actually

01:51:04.590 --> 01:51:06.540
for the Race and Health Observatory.

01:51:06.540 --> 01:51:09.356
And over to you, the
key highlights really,

01:51:09.356 --> 01:51:11.850
and then we can have a conversation.

01:51:11.850 --> 01:51:12.683
- Certainly.

01:51:12.683 --> 01:51:15.392
So thank you very much
for having us at the board

01:51:15.392 --> 01:51:18.607
and delighted to be
joined by Dr. Habib Naqvi,

01:51:18.607 --> 01:51:22.601
who is the director for the NHS
Race and Health Observatory.

01:51:22.601 --> 01:51:25.050
I just want to start by
saying thank you, Amanda,

01:51:25.050 --> 01:51:27.300
for the opportunity to be here.

01:51:27.300 --> 01:51:30.450
We were both at the RHO
board a few weeks ago

01:51:30.450 --> 01:51:33.012
where you undertook that this report

01:51:33.012 --> 01:51:36.120
could come to this board and
it's incredibly important

01:51:36.120 --> 01:51:38.040
that we've had that opportunity.

01:51:38.040 --> 01:51:39.810
So I'll kick us off
and then I'll hand over

01:51:39.810 --> 01:51:42.888
to Habib if I may, and I speak in my role

01:51:42.888 --> 01:51:45.580
as NHS England's Senior
Responsible Officer

01:51:45.580 --> 01:51:48.060
for the Race and Health Observatory.

01:51:48.060 --> 01:51:50.190
And what does that mean in practise?

01:51:50.190 --> 01:51:52.710
So my role is to ensure alignment

01:51:52.710 --> 01:51:55.410
between the RHO's work packages

01:51:55.410 --> 01:51:58.740
and NHS England's strategic priorities.

01:51:58.740 --> 01:52:03.120
It's to assure delivery against
our contractual agreements

01:52:03.120 --> 01:52:05.247
with the Race and Health Observatory,

01:52:05.247 --> 01:52:08.044
to hold and disperse the RHO's budget,

01:52:08.044 --> 01:52:10.140
which sits within the
healthcare inequalities

01:52:10.140 --> 01:52:12.210
improvement program's budget line.

01:52:12.210 --> 01:52:15.690
And finally, to support the
RHO as they deliver their work

01:52:15.690 --> 01:52:18.732
with other partners,
including NHS England,

01:52:18.732 --> 01:52:21.570
the wider NHS and system partners.

01:52:21.570 --> 01:52:24.567
So just to sort of clarify
the arrangements between us

01:52:24.567 --> 01:52:26.670
and the Race and Health Observatory.

01:52:26.670 --> 01:52:29.490
And with that I'll hand over
to Habib to take you through

01:52:29.490 --> 01:52:32.512
the excellent work I'm sure
you can see from the paper--

01:52:32.512 --> 01:52:33.751
- [Richard] Yeah, it's a very good paper.

01:52:33.751 --> 01:52:34.860
- That the RHO has been driving

01:52:34.860 --> 01:52:37.170
in the first year of its existence.

01:52:37.170 --> 01:52:38.310
- Thank you, thank you so much Bola

01:52:38.310 --> 01:52:40.350
and thank you Chair and Amanda.

01:52:40.350 --> 01:52:44.373
I'll just highlight some of
the summary points if I may,

01:52:45.660 --> 01:52:47.880
the first point being
that the establishment

01:52:47.880 --> 01:52:50.400
of the observatory was
announced by NHS England

01:52:50.400 --> 01:52:52.350
prior to the COVID pandemic.

01:52:52.350 --> 01:52:54.300
I think that's an important
point to keep in mind,

01:52:54.300 --> 01:52:57.330
showing the level of kind of foresight

01:52:57.330 --> 01:53:00.990
and leadership shown by the organisation.

01:53:00.990 --> 01:53:03.390
Second point being around
our operating model,

01:53:03.390 --> 01:53:06.000
which is based upon three stages,

01:53:06.000 --> 01:53:07.380
bringing together the evidence base

01:53:07.380 --> 01:53:09.510
around ethnic health inequalities,

01:53:09.510 --> 01:53:10.710
turning that into actionable

01:53:10.710 --> 01:53:13.800
policy recommendations for change.

01:53:13.800 --> 01:53:16.500
And thirdly, supporting
the system on the ground

01:53:16.500 --> 01:53:20.715
as organisations, trusts and
ICSs as we now move forward

01:53:20.715 --> 01:53:23.943
to implement those policies on the ground.

01:53:25.200 --> 01:53:27.832
But it's not just about outlining

01:53:27.832 --> 01:53:29.670
the scale of the challenge,

01:53:29.670 --> 01:53:33.570
but also highlighting some
of the practical solutions.

01:53:33.570 --> 01:53:37.791
And that's the fundamental
point around the establishment

01:53:37.791 --> 01:53:42.480
of an observatory that
achieved its balance

01:53:42.480 --> 01:53:46.110
in terms of modelling
the independent nature

01:53:46.110 --> 01:53:47.717
of the organisation,

01:53:47.717 --> 01:53:51.261
but also working in close
collaboration with policy leads

01:53:51.261 --> 01:53:55.647
within NHS England and other ARBs as well.

01:53:55.647 --> 01:53:57.660
And as Bola has highlighted,

01:53:57.660 --> 01:54:01.140
alignment of our work programme
against the long term plan

01:54:01.140 --> 01:54:05.460
helps to facilitate that
particular relationship.

01:54:05.460 --> 01:54:09.060
When we started the work
just over 12 months ago,

01:54:09.060 --> 01:54:12.000
we came away from an engagement exercise

01:54:12.000 --> 01:54:14.460
with people and organisations wanting us

01:54:14.460 --> 01:54:18.960
to do many things, a long
list of asks essentially,

01:54:18.960 --> 01:54:22.530
which we captured in our work
programme that enables us

01:54:22.530 --> 01:54:24.360
to focus on some of the deep seated

01:54:24.360 --> 01:54:27.233
ethnic health inequalities
around maternal health,

01:54:27.233 --> 01:54:30.090
around mental health,

01:54:30.090 --> 01:54:33.030
but also to focus on some
of the issues of the day.

01:54:33.030 --> 01:54:35.940
So being proactive and reactive as we were

01:54:35.940 --> 01:54:40.560
in terms of COVID-19 vaccination
uptake very early on,

01:54:40.560 --> 01:54:45.560
and now with new models of
care for sickle cell disease.

01:54:46.170 --> 01:54:48.360
But also to keep this agenda

01:54:48.360 --> 01:54:49.770
at the forefront of leaders' minds.

01:54:49.770 --> 01:54:52.050
So there's an element
here about leadership,

01:54:52.050 --> 01:54:54.964
around what is it that
organisations and individuals

01:54:54.964 --> 01:54:58.110
should be doing in this space?

01:54:58.110 --> 01:55:02.700
And finally, in relation to
engagement with our communities,

01:55:02.700 --> 01:55:05.266
sustained and meaningful engagement,

01:55:05.266 --> 01:55:09.120
which is based upon the evidence base

01:55:09.120 --> 01:55:13.547
not just from this country,
but globally as well.

01:55:13.547 --> 01:55:18.547
The paper highlights very
briefly our impact to date

01:55:19.260 --> 01:55:21.630
through driving policy change,
through shaping research,

01:55:21.630 --> 01:55:24.330
engaging the public and
creating practical tools.

01:55:24.330 --> 01:55:25.980
But there's a fifth element
that I'd like to focus on

01:55:25.980 --> 01:55:27.390
and that's influencing leadership.

01:55:27.390 --> 01:55:31.560
And on the back of the US
and the UK government's

01:55:31.560 --> 01:55:34.170
bilateral agreement to twin us

01:55:34.170 --> 01:55:37.713
with the US Centre for Disease
Control and Prevention,

01:55:38.790 --> 01:55:43.790
last year, we have established
a work programme with the CDC

01:55:44.400 --> 01:55:48.107
that enables us not just
to focus on the issues

01:55:48.107 --> 01:55:50.220
within our own geographical borders,

01:55:50.220 --> 01:55:52.230
but to learn from a global perspective

01:55:52.230 --> 01:55:54.960
around some of the common challenges

01:55:54.960 --> 01:55:56.310
and some of the common solutions

01:55:56.310 --> 01:55:57.630
to some of those common challenges

01:55:57.630 --> 01:56:02.220
that we have around race and
ethnic health inequalities.

01:56:02.220 --> 01:56:06.660
Today, we have 18 work
programmes live, up and running

01:56:06.660 --> 01:56:11.660
from looking at reviewing the app

01:56:11.670 --> 01:56:14.460
and diagnostic testing
in neonatal services

01:56:14.460 --> 01:56:16.980
to sickle cell disease as I mentioned

01:56:16.980 --> 01:56:18.978
to looking at long COVID
amongst our ethnic minority

01:56:18.978 --> 01:56:20.640
health and care staff,

01:56:20.640 --> 01:56:23.490
as well as looking at
ensuring digital technology

01:56:23.490 --> 01:56:25.620
as the previous items highlighted

01:56:25.620 --> 01:56:27.690
is inclusive and accessible,

01:56:27.690 --> 01:56:30.150
looking at equality impact
assessments at a very early stage

01:56:30.150 --> 01:56:33.537
within those digital
technologies as we move forward

01:56:33.537 --> 01:56:35.682
and working with Tim's team

01:56:35.682 --> 01:56:38.853
on that particular programme as well.

01:56:39.780 --> 01:56:41.703
And finally, before I hand back,

01:56:42.661 --> 01:56:45.480
the international focus is
really important for us.

01:56:45.480 --> 01:56:49.200
And today marks the
first day of our two day

01:56:49.200 --> 01:56:51.690
international conference
where we've managed

01:56:51.690 --> 01:56:53.460
to bring together race and health experts

01:56:53.460 --> 01:56:55.500
from across the globe, from Australia,

01:56:55.500 --> 01:56:58.800
New Zealand, South Africa,
Guatemala, US and Canada,

01:56:58.800 --> 01:57:00.660
all in one place in London.

01:57:00.660 --> 01:57:04.698
And I've just come from
that conference just now,

01:57:04.698 --> 01:57:08.370
but this is about how we can work together

01:57:08.370 --> 01:57:11.910
and not be focused just kind
of within our own boundaries,

01:57:11.910 --> 01:57:13.080
but look at some of that, you know,

01:57:13.080 --> 01:57:14.490
sharing some of that good practise

01:57:14.490 --> 01:57:17.140
and that replicable good
practise as we move forward.

01:57:18.093 --> 01:57:18.926
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:57:18.926 --> 01:57:19.759
- Thank you, Habib.

01:57:19.759 --> 01:57:22.617
And so we commend the
paper to you and Chair

01:57:22.617 --> 01:57:24.868
and we welcome questions.

01:57:24.868 --> 01:57:26.428
- So let me just, thank you.

01:57:26.428 --> 01:57:27.706
Thank you Bola and thank you Habib.

01:57:27.706 --> 01:57:29.760
So I don't see any comments.

01:57:29.760 --> 01:57:30.593
Amanda, do you want to make a?

01:57:30.593 --> 01:57:32.883
Or David, you have a comment.

01:57:33.930 --> 01:57:35.030
You go first and then.

01:57:37.208 --> 01:57:38.760
- A comment on the question.

01:57:38.760 --> 01:57:40.860
So congratulations Habib and Bola.

01:57:40.860 --> 01:57:44.490
I think to go from a standing start

01:57:44.490 --> 01:57:47.970
to some of the content of the report,

01:57:47.970 --> 01:57:50.100
so much so that you've
got two paragraph tens,

01:57:50.100 --> 01:57:50.980
which I appreciate.

01:57:52.650 --> 01:57:54.957
- [Richard] You've always
been numerate David.

01:57:54.957 --> 01:57:58.500
- But the serious point
is I really liked the fact

01:57:58.500 --> 01:58:00.990
that you didn't just say
what you'd done, the process,

01:58:00.990 --> 01:58:03.700
but you said what the impact
and the outcomes have been.

01:58:03.700 --> 01:58:04.830
And I just wonder whether

01:58:04.830 --> 01:58:07.020
that might be a standard
that we want to set

01:58:07.020 --> 01:58:09.404
through some of these
reports going forward.

01:58:09.404 --> 01:58:11.313
I thought that was terrific.

01:58:12.240 --> 01:58:13.710
So that was my comment.

01:58:13.710 --> 01:58:15.180
I really appreciate the work

01:58:15.180 --> 01:58:18.823
and I just underscore that
international learning as well.

01:58:18.823 --> 01:58:21.210
I do think sometimes we
look inwards too often

01:58:21.210 --> 01:58:22.560
as opposed to looking outwards

01:58:22.560 --> 01:58:24.630
about what's happening
globally and in other places.

01:58:24.630 --> 01:58:26.553
So terrific.

01:58:27.450 --> 01:58:28.980
Can you just, my question is,

01:58:28.980 --> 01:58:31.710
can you just remind us
what the dimensions are?

01:58:31.710 --> 01:58:33.950
The one aspect I would've
liked in an annual report

01:58:33.950 --> 01:58:35.970
is how much and how many people

01:58:35.970 --> 01:58:37.803
you've got doing the work.

01:58:39.060 --> 01:58:42.265
I don't know, maybe I should
know, but I don't know.

01:58:42.265 --> 01:58:43.098
- So hold the--

01:58:43.098 --> 01:58:46.080
I'm going to go around again,
the same 'cos otherwise,

01:58:46.080 --> 01:58:48.450
so hold the questions 'cos I can also see

01:58:48.450 --> 01:58:51.930
that Chris has a question
and Ruth and Munir,

01:58:51.930 --> 01:58:53.970
but we need to move really
quite quickly on time

01:58:53.970 --> 01:58:55.710
because it's a public board.

01:58:55.710 --> 01:58:58.063
So Chris, do you want to go first?

01:58:58.063 --> 01:59:00.090
- A very quick observation
rather than a question,

01:59:00.090 --> 01:59:02.010
which is just to make the
point that we don't always

01:59:02.010 --> 01:59:03.360
need to do everything ourselves

01:59:03.360 --> 01:59:05.910
and that actually our
funding organisations

01:59:05.910 --> 01:59:07.410
with excellent partners like our friends

01:59:07.410 --> 01:59:09.900
at the Confederation, our
friends at NHS providers,

01:59:09.900 --> 01:59:11.996
our friends at the Medical Royal Colleges,

01:59:11.996 --> 01:59:13.890
are closer to the front line

01:59:13.890 --> 01:59:16.650
and have a real relationship with them.

01:59:16.650 --> 01:59:18.474
It just offers us some
really good opportunities.

01:59:18.474 --> 01:59:21.030
And I think this is a
really good example of that.

01:59:21.030 --> 01:59:23.190
And just to echo David's
comment, if I may say Habib,

01:59:23.190 --> 01:59:25.710
I think you've done a
really, really good job very,

01:59:25.710 --> 01:59:27.420
very quickly from the standing start.

01:59:27.420 --> 01:59:30.390
And you've clearly I know had
some very significant impact

01:59:30.390 --> 01:59:32.550
in terms of these areas
that have been outlined.

01:59:32.550 --> 01:59:34.740
So I think that's been really impressive.

01:59:34.740 --> 01:59:35.820
- Very good.

01:59:35.820 --> 01:59:36.653
Ruth.

01:59:36.653 --> 01:59:37.486
- Just two points.

01:59:37.486 --> 01:59:38.760
One is to say a huge thank you.

01:59:38.760 --> 01:59:39.593
Thanks for being there to support us

01:59:39.593 --> 01:59:42.990
at yesterday's event with the eight Bs

01:59:42.990 --> 01:59:45.030
across the whole of
England coming together

01:59:45.030 --> 01:59:48.570
to think about how we
support and put into action.

01:59:48.570 --> 01:59:49.620
Thank you hugely.

01:59:49.620 --> 01:59:51.960
And if we can get some more support

01:59:51.960 --> 01:59:54.150
and more support particularly
with the networks

01:59:54.150 --> 01:59:56.040
like the Jabali Network and the like,

01:59:56.040 --> 01:59:58.620
I'd be really grateful, but thank you.

01:59:58.620 --> 02:00:00.060
- And Munir.

02:00:00.060 --> 02:00:00.893
- Yeah, great working.

02:00:00.893 --> 02:00:03.630
Congratulations on the report.

02:00:03.630 --> 02:00:06.766
Just understanding what
you are going to be doing

02:00:06.766 --> 02:00:09.060
in terms of your work plan going forward

02:00:09.060 --> 02:00:10.560
in terms of engaging with the patients

02:00:10.560 --> 02:00:12.910
and the public in terms
of what they also want.

02:00:14.490 --> 02:00:17.102
- So do you want to answer those?

02:00:17.102 --> 02:00:19.350
I think there were a couple
of questions in there still,

02:00:19.350 --> 02:00:20.460
if you want to answer those.

02:00:20.460 --> 02:00:21.293
- Yeah, sure.

02:00:21.293 --> 02:00:22.830
So in terms of people,

02:00:22.830 --> 02:00:25.891
I think that was directed
in terms of the workforce

02:00:25.891 --> 02:00:28.290
within the observatory itself.

02:00:28.290 --> 02:00:31.058
There are seven of us
within the observatory,

02:00:31.058 --> 02:00:36.058
which included includes
administrative staff as well,

02:00:36.510 --> 02:00:39.780
but we have a fantastic
architecture in terms of our board

02:00:39.780 --> 02:00:41.520
and board members that
really want to roll up

02:00:41.520 --> 02:00:45.344
this race issue, and our
advisory groups as well,

02:00:45.344 --> 02:00:47.520
some of which are chair or co-chaired

02:00:47.520 --> 02:00:50.583
by colleagues from NHS England.

02:00:51.548 --> 02:00:53.430
The Jabali Network have reached out

02:00:53.430 --> 02:00:56.100
and we are working with
them going forward.

02:00:56.100 --> 02:01:00.450
And engagement is a very
important point as well Munir

02:01:00.450 --> 02:01:02.100
and sustain a meaningful engagement.

02:01:02.100 --> 02:01:04.200
And we've done that right from the outset.

02:01:04.200 --> 02:01:07.050
In fact, there's a survey
that has just closed

02:01:07.050 --> 02:01:09.690
looking at the concept
of trust and confidence

02:01:09.690 --> 02:01:11.940
in primary care that our ethnic minority

02:01:11.940 --> 02:01:13.525
and diverse communities have,

02:01:13.525 --> 02:01:18.525
which we are liaising with
Nikita Kanani on specifically

02:01:19.260 --> 02:01:20.403
in terms of particularly coming out

02:01:20.403 --> 02:01:23.580
from the different phases
of the pandemic to make sure

02:01:23.580 --> 02:01:28.140
that we are inclusive to our
diverse communities as well.

02:01:28.140 --> 02:01:31.485
- Thank you, can I let Amanda
make the last comment on this?

02:01:31.485 --> 02:01:32.318
- Thank you.

02:01:32.318 --> 02:01:33.151
Thank you, Richard.

02:01:33.151 --> 02:01:36.046
And thank you, Habib and Bola.

02:01:36.046 --> 02:01:39.930
I just have three observations
that I wanted to make.

02:01:39.930 --> 02:01:43.410
I think you've had incredibly
positive feedback on the paper

02:01:43.410 --> 02:01:46.533
and deservedly so, but why
for me, there are three things

02:01:46.533 --> 02:01:49.230
that I just really wanted to emphasise.

02:01:49.230 --> 02:01:52.320
I think the thing that is so
powerful about the approach

02:01:52.320 --> 02:01:55.590
you've taken is the focus
on things that matter.

02:01:55.590 --> 02:01:58.230
So it is a lesson for us as
we think about, you know,

02:01:58.230 --> 02:02:00.300
the number of different things
we're trying to prioritise

02:02:00.300 --> 02:02:02.130
as we go through the LTP update.

02:02:02.130 --> 02:02:03.750
The fact that you've aligned with the LTP,

02:02:03.750 --> 02:02:04.890
but you've got that opportunity

02:02:04.890 --> 02:02:07.920
to then also be reactive
to stuff that happens.

02:02:07.920 --> 02:02:11.100
And you know, to share
with the board as well,

02:02:11.100 --> 02:02:14.010
I mentioned this at Expo Confed recently.

02:02:14.010 --> 02:02:16.830
And I went out to talk to a
group of sickle cell patients

02:02:16.830 --> 02:02:20.550
who were really clear and really powerful

02:02:20.550 --> 02:02:21.576
about their lived experience.

02:02:21.576 --> 02:02:24.870
And one of them said to me, you know,

02:02:24.870 --> 02:02:27.540
would I have been treated
like this if I was white?

02:02:27.540 --> 02:02:30.750
So these are real issues.

02:02:30.750 --> 02:02:33.300
And I just really want
to commend the focus

02:02:33.300 --> 02:02:34.133
'cos it is about,

02:02:34.133 --> 02:02:35.610
you know, you could do a
hundred million things,

02:02:35.610 --> 02:02:38.280
but the alignment around
the LTP priorities

02:02:38.280 --> 02:02:40.770
and then that focus on the
things that really matter.

02:02:40.770 --> 02:02:43.613
Number one, number two,
just to echo what Ruth said,

02:02:43.613 --> 02:02:47.820
I think the way that the
RHO links in proactively

02:02:47.820 --> 02:02:51.300
with our policy teams and also
now increasingly with ICSs

02:02:51.300 --> 02:02:54.270
is the heart of what makes this
a successful way of working

02:02:54.270 --> 02:02:56.130
I think for me, because it is not about,

02:02:56.130 --> 02:02:58.140
there's an interesting report
that's going to sit over there

02:02:58.140 --> 02:02:59.310
and it's going to be something we get to

02:02:59.310 --> 02:03:00.630
late on a Thursday afternoon.

02:03:00.630 --> 02:03:03.180
It's no, no, it's knitted in to the way

02:03:03.180 --> 02:03:05.220
that the programmes are developed,
the way that they're run.

02:03:05.220 --> 02:03:07.740
And increasingly I think
we'll see that replicated

02:03:07.740 --> 02:03:08.690
with the ICSs.

02:03:08.690 --> 02:03:10.710
So it's equipping leaders with the skills

02:03:10.710 --> 02:03:11.910
and the information and the knowledge

02:03:11.910 --> 02:03:14.070
so they can really make
a difference locally.

02:03:14.070 --> 02:03:16.140
And then finally, I just
wanted to commend the focus

02:03:16.140 --> 02:03:17.520
on impact because I think Bola,

02:03:17.520 --> 02:03:19.170
this has been something
you talk a lot about

02:03:19.170 --> 02:03:21.060
in terms of health inequalities.

02:03:21.060 --> 02:03:24.645
It's not about just
understanding what the data

02:03:24.645 --> 02:03:27.000
is telling us or just
having a theoretical answer.

02:03:27.000 --> 02:03:29.370
It's does it in the real
world make a difference?

02:03:29.370 --> 02:03:32.160
And I think it's a really powerful way

02:03:32.160 --> 02:03:33.540
of focusing in the work

02:03:33.540 --> 02:03:35.910
and galvanising actually
what you have done,

02:03:35.910 --> 02:03:39.420
which is a huge both national
and international community

02:03:39.420 --> 02:03:43.230
now around really turning
what you've already done

02:03:43.230 --> 02:03:45.480
into the maximum impact,

02:03:45.480 --> 02:03:48.150
but also a really exciting
programme for the future.

02:03:48.150 --> 02:03:49.179
- So thank you very much.

02:03:49.179 --> 02:03:51.990
It's a very good annual report.

02:03:51.990 --> 02:03:55.443
So what I'd like to just
draw the board to a close.

02:03:57.390 --> 02:03:59.610
I think we've had some really
excellent discussions today.

02:03:59.610 --> 02:04:03.540
This morning was really busy
and it built on a lot of work

02:04:03.540 --> 02:04:04.920
that goes on between the boards

02:04:04.920 --> 02:04:07.020
in the various subcommittees of the board.

02:04:07.020 --> 02:04:09.630
So my thanks to colleagues
exec and non-exec

02:04:09.630 --> 02:04:13.140
for all of their contribution
between these board meetings.

02:04:13.140 --> 02:04:14.784
But we've had some excellent discussions.

02:04:14.784 --> 02:04:16.620
It's also quite clear that we are under

02:04:16.620 --> 02:04:20.649
significant pressure, operational
and financial pressure.

02:04:20.649 --> 02:04:25.649
And so we have to continue
to work really hard at those.

02:04:26.910 --> 02:04:29.250
It's an important day I think actually,

02:04:29.250 --> 02:04:30.300
as we've laid out and Mark,

02:04:30.300 --> 02:04:32.100
thank you for your executive leadership

02:04:32.100 --> 02:04:36.360
working close with Wol on the
merge of the integration plans

02:04:36.360 --> 02:04:37.953
for the new NHS England.

02:04:39.000 --> 02:04:40.650
So it is a really important day

02:04:40.650 --> 02:04:42.150
and now there's much more to do

02:04:42.150 --> 02:04:44.730
to get that brought into effect.

02:04:44.730 --> 02:04:46.380
And Amanda, just to thank you

02:04:46.380 --> 02:04:48.150
for your authoritative leadership.

02:04:48.150 --> 02:04:53.150
Actually, you've sort
of grown up in the NHS.

02:04:54.780 --> 02:04:58.560
And so actually, your
perspective and reflection

02:04:58.560 --> 02:05:01.380
on how we can change the systems

02:05:01.380 --> 02:05:03.507
or how we can change
the way the NHS operates

02:05:03.507 --> 02:05:04.860
to be this new NHS England

02:05:04.860 --> 02:05:07.710
I think has been really
powerful, so thank you for that.

02:05:07.710 --> 02:05:09.900
So I'm going to quickly look around.

02:05:09.900 --> 02:05:12.420
I'm not catching anybody's
eyes, given time,

02:05:12.420 --> 02:05:14.310
as to whether there's any other business

02:05:14.310 --> 02:05:15.146
that anyone wants to raise.

02:05:15.146 --> 02:05:16.770
And if not, just to thank everybody

02:05:16.770 --> 02:05:18.543
for what's been a really busy day.

